\subsection{Property~\textup{(T)} of the base}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:literal-base-T}
The twenty-relator group $\mathcal B$ has property~\textup{(T)}, in both
the real-orthogonal and complex-unitary formulations.
\leanverified{Sofic/LiteralBaseP13PropertyTBridge}{GroupApproximation.LiteralBaseP13PropertyTBridge.manuscriptBaseHasKazhdanPropertyT}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Step 1: a certified Steinberg-type presentation.}  Let $P_{13}$ be
the group on the six generators
$e_{12},e_{13},e_{21},e_{23},e_{31},e_{32}$ with the thirteen relators
\[
\begin{gathered}
 [e_{12},e_{23}]=e_{13},\qquad [e_{13},e_{32}]=e_{12},\qquad
 [e_{21},e_{13}]=e_{23},\\
 [e_{23},e_{31}]=e_{21},\qquad [e_{31},e_{12}]=e_{32},\qquad
 [e_{32},e_{21}]=e_{31},\\
 [e_{12},e_{13}]=[e_{12},e_{32}]=[e_{13},e_{23}]
 =[e_{21},e_{23}]=[e_{21},e_{31}]=[e_{31},e_{32}]=1,\\
 (e_{12}\,e_{21}^{-1}\,e_{12})^{4}=1,
\end{gathered}
\]
the elementary-matrix relations in rank three.  The degree-one Hodge
Laplacian $\Delta_1$ of this presentation, a matrix over the real group
ring of $P_{13}$, admits the exact decomposition
\[
 \Delta_1=\tfrac1{250}\,I+Q^{*}Q+R,
\]
in which $Q$ and $R$ are matrices over the group ring with rational
coefficients supported on finitely many group elements, and every row
and every column of $R$ has coefficient $\ell^1$ norm at most
$\tfrac1{500}$.  Clearing denominators makes both sides equal integer
combinations of finitely many group elements, so the decomposition and
the two bounds are finite identities and inequalities over $\mathbb Z$,
evaluated in exact arithmetic; the certificate data are in the
supplementary material.  Together they bound $\Delta_1$ below by
$\tfrac1{500}$ in every orthogonal representation.  Testing that bound
on coboundaries gives the quadratic gap
\[
 \lVert\Delta\xi\rVert^2\ \ge\ \tfrac1{500}\,\langle\Delta\xi,\xi\rangle
\]
for the six-generator Laplacian $\Delta$.  The gap places the spectrum
of $\Delta$ in $\{0\}\cup[\tfrac1{500},\infty)$.  The kernel of
$\Delta$ is exactly the space of invariant vectors.  The localization
makes $0$ an isolated point of the spectrum but does not by itself
exclude it.  In a representation with no nonzero invariant vector the
kernel is trivial, so $\Delta$ is bounded below there.  Hence a unit
vector almost fixed by the six generators has small Laplacian energy
and is close to an invariant vector.  This yields a Kazhdan pair: $P_{13}$ has
property~\textup{(T)}.  Certificates of this kind go back to Ozawa's
algebraic characterization of property~\textup{(T)} \cite{Ozawa} and its
computer-assisted implementations for $\SL_3(\mathbb Z)$
\cite{NetzerThom}.

\emph{Step 2: the explicit surjection onto the linear presentation.}
Map the six generators to the words
\[
\begin{gathered}
 e_{12}\mapsto y^{-1}xzy^{-1}x^{-1},\quad
 e_{13}\mapsto xzy^{-1}x^{-1}y,\quad
 e_{21}\mapsto y^{-1}xyxz,\\
 e_{23}\mapsto x^{-1}y^{-1}xzy^{-1},\quad
 e_{31}\mapsto xy^{-1}xzy^{-1}x,\quad
 e_{32}\mapsto x^{-1}zy^{-1}x^{-1}y^{-1}x^{-1}
\end{gathered}
\]
in the linear presentation $\mathcal R$ of \eqref{eq:sl3presentation}.
Each of the thirteen relators of $P_{13}$ reduces to the identity in
$\mathcal R$ using only the eight relators of
\eqref{eq:sl3presentation}; the thirteen reductions are finite words
and are carried out in the supplementary material.  The assignment
therefore defines a homomorphism $P_{13}\to\mathcal R$, and the same
reductions exhibit explicit words in the six images whose values are
$z$ and $xy$.  Those two elements generate $\mathcal R$: writing $g=z$
and $h=xy$, the eight relators give the generating-pair word of
\cite{CLV}
\[
 y=\bigl(ghgh^{-1}g^{-1}hg^{-1}h^{-1}g^{-1}h^{-1}g^{-1}h\bigr)^{2},
\]
and then $x=hy^{-1}$.  Hence the homomorphism is surjective.
Property~\textup{(T)} passes to quotients, so $\mathcal R$ has
property~\textup{(T)}.

\emph{Step 3: from an almost-invariant vector to an invariant one over
the base.}  The generators
$x,y,z$ of $\mathcal B$ satisfy the relators of $\mathcal R$, so
$\mathcal R$ maps to the linear subgroup of $\mathcal B$ they generate,
and Step~2
gives a finite set $S$ of words in $x,y,z$ and a $\kappa>0$ forming a
Kazhdan pair for it.  A Kazhdan pair remains one when its constant is
decreased, so assume $\kappa\le1$.  Let $\rho$ be an orthogonal representation of
$\mathcal B$ with a unit vector $\xi$ displaced by less than $\kappa/64$ by
every element of the finite control set consisting of $S$ together
with the translations $v_2,v_3$.  Since $v_1=xv_2x^{-1}$ by the
displayed $x$-action, any vector $\eta$ fixed by the linear subgroup
satisfies
$\lVert\rho(v_1)\eta-\eta\rVert=\lVert\rho(v_2)\eta-\eta\rVert$, so
$v_1$ needs no separate control.  We construct a nonzero
invariant vector near $\xi$ as follows.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Projecting $\xi$ to the subspace of vectors fixed by the linear
subgroup gives such a fixed vector $p$ with $\lVert p-\xi\rVert<1/64$, by the
standard estimate that this projection moves a unit vector by at most
the ratio of its displacement under the Kazhdan set to the Kazhdan
constant.
\item Translations move $p$ by a bound independent of the lattice
vector.  Three explicit words in $x,y,z$ act on the translation
lattice as elementary transvections.  Consequently every lattice
element is a product of two conjugates, by elements of the linear
subgroup, of basis translations.  The three words and the elimination
that produces this factorization are finite data of the same kind as the
thirteen reductions of Step~2, and are in the supplementary material.
A conjugate $rvr^{-1}$ with $r$ in the linear subgroup
displaces the fixed vector $p$ exactly as much as $v$ does.  Each
control translation displaces $p$ by less than
$1/64+\kappa/64+1/64=(\kappa+2)/64\le3/64$, its displacement bound at
$\xi$ transferring across $\lVert p-\xi\rVert$ at both ends.
Hence every translation displaces $p$ by at most $1/8$.
\item The orbit of $p$ under the translation subgroup is bounded, so
the Hilbert-space circumcenter argument gives a translation-fixed
vector $q$ with $\lVert q-p\rVert\le1/8$; the orthogonal projection
$\eta$ of $p$ to the translation-fixed subspace is the nearest fixed
vector, so $\lVert\eta-p\rVert\le\lVert q-p\rVert\le1/8$.
The linear subgroup normalizes the translation subgroup and fixes $p$,
so $\eta$ is fixed by it as well.  Translations and the linear
generators generate $\mathcal B$, so $\eta$ is $\mathcal B$-invariant.
Finally, $\lVert \eta-\xi\rVert\le 1/8+1/64<1$ keeps $\eta$ nonzero.
\end{enumerate}
Thus almost-invariant vectors produce invariant vectors and $\mathcal B$
has property~\textup{(T)} in the real-orthogonal formulation;
realification and complexification identify it with the complex-unitary
formulation.
\end{proof}

$P_{13}\cong\SL_3(\mathbb Z)$, and $\mathcal B$ is the affine group
$\mathbb Z^3\rtimes\SL_3(\mathbb Z)$ (Section~\ref{app:p13}).

\section{Nontriviality of \texorpdfstring{$w$}{w}}\label{sec:witness}

Clifford signs, shifts, and HNN doubling have appeared together before in
approximate-representation theory, notably in Slofstra's hyperlinear-profile
construction \cite[Section~2]{SlofstraProfile}.

\begin{construction}[Clifford groups]\label{con:clifford}
For a set $X$, use the real Clifford-algebra convention generated by
$(c_x)_{x\in X}$ with $c_x^2=1$ and $c_xc_y=-c_yc_x$ for $x\ne y$, and let
$\Cl(X)$ be the subgroup of units generated by $-1$ and the $c_x$.
Write $\zeta=-1$.  In this concrete group,
\[
 \zeta^2=c_x^2=1,\qquad c_xc_y=\zeta c_yc_x\quad(x\ne y).
\]
The sign $\zeta$ is nontrivial.  Thus $\Cl(X)$ is a central
extension, by the sign $\zeta$, of the elementary abelian $2$-group on
the points of $X$: the commutator of two distinct generators is
$\zeta$,
unlike the commuting generators of a classical lamplighter.  Every
permutation of $X$ acts on this
group by $c_x\mapsto c_{\sigma(x)}$ and fixes $\zeta$.
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptExactWrappers}{GroupApproximation.ManuscriptExactWrappers.manuscriptCliffordConstruction}
\end{construction}

\begin{lemma}[linear model]\label{lem:linear}
For $M\in\GL_3(\mathbb Q)$ and $u\in\mathbb Q^3$, write
\[
 A(M,u)=\begin{pmatrix}M&u\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Assign $v_i\mapsto A(I_3,e_i)$ and
\[
\begin{aligned}
 x&\mapsto A\!\left(\begin{pmatrix}0&1&0\\0&0&1\\1&0&0\end{pmatrix},0\right),
&y&\mapsto A\!\left(\begin{pmatrix}1&0&1\\0&-1&-1\\0&1&0\end{pmatrix},0\right),\\[3pt]
 z&\mapsto A\!\left(\begin{pmatrix}0&1&0\\1&0&0\\-1&-1&-1\end{pmatrix},0\right).
\end{aligned}
\]
Let $\bar\Gamma\le\GL_4(\mathbb Q)$ be the subgroup generated by these six
matrices, and put
$D=\operatorname{diag}(2,2,2,1)$.  Direct matrix computation proves that
the six matrices satisfy all twenty relators of $\mathcal B$ and that
\[
 \bar\alpha(u)=DuD^{-1}
\]
is an injective endomorphism of $\bar\Gamma$ which squares the three
translation generators and fixes $x,y,z$.  Moreover the matrix assigned to
$v_1$ does not lie in $\operatorname{range}(\bar\alpha)$.
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptExactWrappers}{GroupApproximation.ManuscriptExactWrappers.manuscriptLinearModel}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The twenty relator checks reduce to sixteen rational identities for each
matrix equation.  Conjugation by $D$ gives the six stable-letter equations
and is injective because it is conjugation by an invertible matrix.  Every
displayed generator and its explicitly computed inverse has integral entries;
equivalently, all six generators lie in $\GL_4(\mathbb Z)$.  Hence
$\bar\Gamma\le\GL_4(\mathbb Z)$.  However $D^{-1}\bar v_1D$ has entry $1/2$
in position $(1,4)$, so it is not in $\bar\Gamma$.  This proves the last
assertion.
\end{proof}

Form the direct limit $T=\varinjlim(\bar\Gamma,\bar\alpha)$, the direct limit of $\bar\alpha$, extend it by its shift
$\tau$, and call the resulting group $\Sigma=T\rtimes\mathbb Z$.  Let
$j\colon\bar\Gamma\hookrightarrow\Sigma$ be the level-zero map.  Put
$X=\Sigma/j(\bar\Gamma)$, with base point $o=j(\bar\Gamma)$.  Since
$D^{-1}\bar v_1D\notin\bar\Gamma$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:linear}), we have
$\tau o\ne j(\bar v_1)\tau o$, and the Clifford generators at these two
points anticommute.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:witness}
$w\neq1$ in $E$.
\leanverified{Sofic/LiteralNonMFLinearWitness}{GroupApproximation.LiteralNonMFLinearWitness.literal_mark_ne_one}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Form $W:=\Cl(X)\rtimes\Sigma$ as in Construction~\ref{con:clifford}.
Define a map on the generators of \eqref{eq:E} by
\[
\begin{gathered}
  g\mapsto j(\bar g)\in\Sigma\quad
  (g\in\{v_1,v_2,v_3,x,y,z\}),\\
  t\mapsto\tau,\qquad c\mapsto c_o .
\end{gathered}
\]
The exact checks in Lemma~\ref{lem:linear} give the twenty base and six
stable-letter relations.  The level-zero subgroup fixes $o$, so $c_o$
commutes with every base generator, and $c_o^2=1$.
Under this assignment,
\[
  tct^{-1}\mapsto c_{\tau o},\qquad
  v_1(tct^{-1})v_1^{-1}\mapsto c_{j(\bar v_1)\tau o},
\]
and the two points are distinct by Lemma~\ref{lem:linear}, so
\[
  w\mapsto[c_{\tau o},c_{j(\bar v_1)\tau o}]=-1=\zeta .
\]
Since $\zeta$ is central in $W$, the remaining relations $[w,g]=1$ for
$g\in\{v_1,v_2,v_3,x,y,z,t,c\}$ are also satisfied.  Hence the
assignment extends to a group homomorphism $E\to W$ sending $w$ to
$\zeta\neq1$, and therefore $w\neq1$ in $E$.
\end{proof}

When $X$ is infinite so is $\Cl(X)$, but each of its elements
involves only finitely many Clifford generators, and every element of
$\Sigma$ used above is a rational $4\times4$ matrix.  The two points are
distinct because $D^{-1}\bar v_1D$ has the entry $1/2$ in position
$(1,4)$ and therefore does not lie in $\bar\Gamma\le\GL_4(\mathbb Z)$.

\section{The MF equivalences}\label{app:equivalences}

An \emph{approximate unitary representation} for a countable group $G$, a finite
set $F\subseteq G$, a separation constant $\delta>0$, and a tolerance
$\varepsilon>0$ is a map $V\colon G\to\U(r)$, for some positive
dimension $r$, such that
\begin{gather*}
 \opnorm{V(g)V(h)-V(gh)}\le\varepsilon\quad(g,h\in F),\\
 \opnorm{V(g)-V(h)}\ge\delta\quad(g\ne h\in F).
\end{gather*}
The local formulation in Proposition~\ref{prop:mf-equivalences} asks for
such a model with $\delta=1$ for every finite $F$ and every
$\varepsilon>0$.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:mf-equivalences}]
Lemma~\ref{lem:unitarycorona} identifies
$\prod_n\U(d_n)/\mathcal N$ with $\U(\mathcal Q)$, which is the first
equivalence once the dimension convention is normalized.

\emph{Dimension normalization.}  Given positive dimensions $d_n$,
replace the $n$th sequence of unitary lifts by
\[
 \widetilde V_n(g)
 =I_{\C^{d_0}}\oplus\cdots\oplus I_{\C^{d_{n-1}}}\oplus V_n(g)
\]
on $\C^{d_0}\oplus\cdots\oplus\C^{d_n}$, of strictly increasing
dimension $D_n=d_0+\cdots+d_n$.  Block-diagonal operator norms are
maxima and every earlier block is the same identity for all group
elements, so for all $g,h$
\begin{align*}
 \opnorm{\widetilde V_n(g)\widetilde V_n(h)-\widetilde V_n(gh)}
 &=\opnorm{V_n(g)V_n(h)-V_n(gh)},\\
 \opnorm{\widetilde V_n(g)-\widetilde V_n(h)}
 &=\opnorm{V_n(g)-V_n(h)}.
\end{align*}
Both the class in the quotient and its injectivity are therefore
unchanged, and the converse is immediate.

Suppose first that
$\rho\colon G\to\U(\mathcal Q((d_n)))$ is injective, and choose unitary lifts
$u_n(g)$ of $\rho(g)$.  Fix a finite set $F\subseteq G$ and $\varepsilon>0$.
For each ordered pair $g\ne h$ in $F$, injectivity says that
$(u_n(g)^{-1}u_n(h))_n$ does not converge to the identity in operator norm.
Hence there are $\delta_{g,h}>0$ and arbitrarily large coordinates at which
\[
 \lVert u_n(g)-u_n(h)\rVert\ge\delta_{g,h}.
\]
Choose an integer $N_{g,h}$ with $8<N_{g,h}\,\delta_{g,h}^2$.  The
following estimate then applies: if $u,v\in\U(d)$ satisfy
$\opnorm{u-v}\ge\delta$, then $\opnorm{u^{\otimes p}-v^{\otimes p}}>1$
for some $p\le N$ whenever $8<N\delta^{2}$.  Indeed, put $z=u^{*}v$, so
that $\opnorm{u-v}=\opnorm{1-z}$ and $z$ has a spectral value
$e^{2\pi i\alpha}$ with $|1-e^{2\pi i\alpha}|=2\sin(\pi\alpha')\ge\delta$,
where $\alpha'=\min(\{\alpha\},1-\{\alpha\})$; since
$(u^{\otimes p})^{*}v^{\otimes p}=z^{\otimes p}$ has $e^{2\pi ip\alpha}$
in its spectrum,
\[
 \opnorm{u^{\otimes p}-v^{\otimes p}}
 \;\ge\;\bigl|1-e^{2\pi ip\alpha}\bigr|
 \;=\;2\sin\bigl(\pi\min(\{p\alpha\},1-\{p\alpha\})\bigr),
\]
which exceeds $1$ as soon as $\{p\alpha\}$ lies in $(1/6,5/6)$.  The
bound $2\sin(\pi\alpha')\ge\delta$ gives $\alpha'\ge\delta/(2\pi)$;
consecutive multiples of $\alpha$ move $\{p\alpha\}$ by $\alpha'$ in a
fixed direction modulo $1$, which is less than the length $2/3$ of that
interval, so some $p\le\lceil1/(6\alpha')\rceil\le\pi/(3\delta)+1<N$
lands in it.
The quotient homomorphism law makes all multiplicative defects for pairs in
$F$ arbitrarily small at every sufficiently large coordinate.  Choose, for
each $g\ne h$, a coordinate with the displayed separation and with every
required defect at most $\varepsilon/N_{g,h}$.  Then the $p$th tensor
power separates the designated pair by more than $1$, while the
tensor-product telescoping identity bounds each new defect by
$p\,\varepsilon/N_{g,h}\le\varepsilon$.  Finally take the block
sum of these finitely many pair-specific models.  Operator norms of block
diagonal matrices are maxima, so every pair in $F$ is separated by at least
$1$ and every required product has defect at most $\varepsilon$.  This gives
the normalized approximate representation.

Conversely, suppose approximate unitary representations with separation
constant $1$ exist for all finite sets and tolerances.  Choose models
along an exhaustion with errors tending to zero.  Each fixed pair
$g,h$ is then eventually represented multiplicatively, and each fixed pair
$g\ne h$ is eventually separated by $1$.  Hence the classes define an
injective homomorphism into $\prod_n\U(r_n)/\mathcal N$, with no
condition on the dimension sequence.  In particular every nonidentity
element is separated by some homomorphism to a matrix quotient, so the
MF residual of
Definition~\ref{def:radical} is trivial, and the block-sum diagonalization
in the proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:univquot}(2) then assembles the
separating sequences into a single faithful homomorphism to
$\U(\mathcal Q)$; that diagonalization uses the dimension
normalization above and nothing else from the present proposition.

\end{proof}

\section{The finite-stage proof}\label{app:finite-stage}

Theorem~\ref{thm:kazhdan-transport} also has a proof at finite stages,
in which every estimate is an explicit inequality in $\kappa$, $|S|$,
the uniform bound on $(x_n)$, and the multiplicative defects of $U_n$,
conditional on the spectral-gap estimate~(i) below.  That estimate is
not reducible to a threshold on finitely many defects:
property~\textup{(T)} constrains exact unitary representations, while
at a finite stage the matrix models generate only a representation of
the free group on $S$.  Making it effective as well would need a
sum-of-squares certificate for property~\textup{(T)} in the sense of
\cite{Ozawa}.

\begin{proof}[Finite-stage proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:kazhdan-transport}]
Property~\textup{(T)} provides a finite symmetric generating set
$S\subseteq\Gamma$ with $1\in S$ and a constant $\kappa\in(0,1]$ such
that $(S,\kappa)$ is a Kazhdan pair.  As in the proof of
Section~\ref{sec:transport}, each group element acts by conjugation,
$\Ad U_n(g)\,\xi=U_n(g)\,\xi\,U_n(g)^*$, on
$L^2(M_{d_n}(\C),\tr_{d_n})$, and operator-norm almost multiplicativity
of $U_n$ makes $g\mapsto\Ad U_n(g)$ an operator-norm asymptotic
representation on these Hilbert spaces.

\emph{The almost-fixed spectral subspace.}  Let $H_n$ be the Hermitian part of the
average $\frac1{|S|}\sum_{s'\in S}\Ad U_n(\iota(s'))$, fix the threshold
\[
 \theta=\tfrac12\Bigl(\Bigl(1-\tfrac{\kappa^2}{4|S|}\Bigr)+1\Bigr)<1,
\]
and let $P_n$ be the spectral projection of $H_n$ for the part of the
spectrum above $\theta$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{Displacement.}  For every $\gamma\in\Gamma$,
$\opnorm{(\Ad U_n(\iota(\gamma))-1)\,P_n}\to0$.  This comes from a
quantitative spectral-gap estimate: for every pair $a,b$ with
$1-\kappa^2/(4|S|)<a$ and $b<1$, once the multiplicative defects of
$U_n$ on the finitely many group elements involved are small enough,
no eigenvalue of $H_n$ lies in $[a,b]$.  Indeed, a unit eigenvector
$\eta$ with $H_n\eta=\lambda\eta$ satisfies
\[
 \frac1{|S|}\sum_{s'\in S}
   \hsnorm{(\Ad U_n(\iota(s'))-1)\eta}^{2}=2(1-\lambda),
\]
so $\lambda\ge a>1-\kappa^{2}/(4|S|)$ forces
$\hsnorm{(\Ad U_n(\iota(s'))-1)\eta}^{2}<\kappa^{2}/2$ for every
$s'\in S$; the Kazhdan inequality then places $\eta$ near an invariant
vector, on which $H_n$ is within the multiplicative defects of $1$,
against $\lambda\le b<1$.
\leanverified{Sofic/WeakMFVectorGNS}{GroupApproximation.WeakMFVectorGNS.hermitianAverage_eventually_no_intermediate_eigenvalues}
Hence the spectrum of $H_n$ above $\theta$ is eventually within any
prescribed $\delta$ of $1$, so a unit vector $\eta\in\operatorname{ran}P_n$
has $\langle(1-H_n)\eta,\eta\rangle\le\delta$; since $1-H_n$ is the
average of the positive operators $1-\operatorname{Re}\Ad U_n(\iota(s'))$,
each generator satisfies
$\tfrac1{2}\hsnorm{(\Ad U_n(\iota(s'))-1)\eta}^2\le|S|\,\delta$, and a word
in $S$ of length $\ell$ moves $\eta$ by at most $\ell\sqrt{2|S|\delta}$
plus the accumulated multiplicative defects.
\item[(ii)] \emph{Spectral concentration.}  Write $\xi_n\in L^2(M_{d_n}(\C),\tr_{d_n})$
for the vector $x_n$.  The commutator hypothesis says each
$\Ad U_n(\iota(s'))$ almost fixes $\xi_n$, so the residual
$(1-H_n)\xi_n$ is Hilbert--Schmidt null.  On
$\operatorname{ran}(1-P_n)$ the operator $1-H_n$ is bounded below by
$1-\theta$, and it commutes with $P_n$, so every vector $\eta$
satisfies
\[
 \hsnorm{(1-P_n)\eta}\;\le\;(1-\theta)^{-1}\,\hsnorm{(1-H_n)\eta}.
\]
Applied to $\eta=\xi_n$, this shows $\xi_n$ is asymptotically
concentrated in $\operatorname{ran}P_n$.
\end{enumerate}

\emph{Reversing the one-sided projection estimate.}  Put
$T_n=\Ad U_n(s)$ and $Q_n=T_nP_nT_n^*$, a projection of the same rank as
$P_n$.  Since $\opnorm{(1-Q_n)P_n}=\opnorm{(1-P_n)T_n^{*}P_n}$, it is
enough to bound the latter.  Let $\eta\in\operatorname{ran}P_n$ be a
unit vector and let $s'\in S$.  The hypothesis
$s\iota(\Gamma)s^{-1}\subseteq\iota(\Gamma)$ supplies a single
$\delta_{s'}\in\Gamma$ with $s\iota(s')s^{-1}=\iota(\delta_{s'})$, and
\[
 \bigl(\Ad U_n(\iota(s'))-1\bigr)T_n^{*}\eta
 \;=\;T_n^{*}\bigl(\Ad U_n(\iota(\delta_{s'}))-1\bigr)\eta
 \;+\;R_{n,s'},
\]
where $\opnorm{R_{n,s'}}$ is bounded by the multiplicative defects of
$U_n$ on $\{s,\iota(s'),s^{-1}\}$ and therefore tends to $0$.  By~(i)
so does the first term, so $T_n^{*}\eta$ is asymptotically fixed by
every generator, and~(ii) places it asymptotically in
$\operatorname{ran}P_n$.  Hence
$\opnorm{(1-Q_n)P_n}\to0$, the finite-stage form of the one-sided
inclusion $P\le VPV^*$.  Since $\operatorname{rank}P_n=
\operatorname{rank}Q_n$, the equal-rank projection lemma --- for projections $p,q$ of
equal finite rank, $\opnorm{(1-q)p}\le\varepsilon<1$ implies
$\opnorm{(1-p)q}\le\varepsilon/\sqrt{1-\varepsilon^2}$ --- reverses the
containment:
\[
 \opnorm{(1-P_n)\,Q_n}\longrightarrow0.
\]

\emph{Combining the estimates.}  Fix $\gamma$ and $\varepsilon>0$, let $M\ge1$ bound
$\opnorm{x_n}$, and put $q=\min(1,\varepsilon/(40(M^2+1)))$.  For all
large $n$,
\[
 \hsnorm{(1-P_n)\xi_n}^2\le q,\qquad
 \opnorm{(1-P_n)Q_n}\le q,\qquad
 \opnorm{(A_n-1)P_n}\le q,
\]
where $A_n=\Ad U_n(\iota(\gamma))$.  Decompose the transported vector
along $P_n$ and $Q_n$:
\[
 T_n\xi_n\;=\;P_nQ_n\,T_n\xi_n\;+\;(1-P_n)Q_n\,T_n\xi_n
 \;+\;(1-Q_n)\,T_n\xi_n.
\]
Since $Q_n=T_nP_nT_n^*$ and $T_n$ is conjugation by a unitary,
\[
 \hsnorm{(1-Q_n)T_n\xi_n}=\hsnorm{T_n(1-P_n)\xi_n}
 =\hsnorm{(1-P_n)\xi_n}\le q^{1/2};
\]
the middle term obeys
$\hsnorm{(1-P_n)Q_n\,T_n\xi_n}\le\opnorm{(1-P_n)Q_n}\hsnorm{\xi_n}
\le qM$, and the displacement estimate moves the first term by at most
$q\hsnorm{\xi_n}\le qM$.  Applying $A_n-1$, whose operator norm is at
most $2$, to the last two terms and using the displacement bound on
the first gives
\[
 \hsnorm{(A_n-1)\,T_n\xi_n}^2
 \;\le\;\bigl(qM+2qM+2q^{1/2}\bigr)^2
 \;\le\;18q^2M^2+16q\;\le\;\varepsilon.
\]
Since $T_n\xi_n$ is the vector of $U_n(s)x_nU_n(s)^*$, this proves the
asserted convergence.  Once the threshold of~(i) is fixed, every bound
used above is explicit in $\kappa$, $|S|$, $M$, and the multiplicative
defects of $U_n$ on the finitely many group elements involved.
\end{proof}

\clearpage

\section{Transport at arbitrary weights}\label{supp:transport-variants}

The transport theorem extends to arbitrary weights $\nu_n$ in place of
the dimension; Theorem~\ref{thm:kazhdan-transport} is the case
$\nu_n=d_n$.

\begin{theorem}[transport at an arbitrary weight, and of intertwiners]
\label{thm:transport-variants}
Let $\Gamma$, $H$, $\iota$, and $s$ be as in
Theorem~\ref{thm:kazhdan-transport}.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Let $(U_n)$ be as there, let $(\nu_n)$ be nonnegative weights,
and suppose there is $C$ with
$\operatorname{Tr}(x_n^*x_n)\le C\,\nu_n$ for all $n$, and that for
every $\gamma\in\Gamma$ and every $\varepsilon>0$, eventually
\[
 \operatorname{Tr}\,\bigl|x_n-U_n(\iota(\gamma))\,x_n\,
   U_n(\iota(\gamma))^*\bigr|^2\le\varepsilon\,\nu_n.
\]
Then $U_n(s)\,x_nU_n(s)^*$ and $U_n(s)^*\,x_nU_n(s)$ satisfy both
conditions as well.
\item Let $U^1_n\colon H\to\U(d^1_n)$ and
$U^2_n\colon H\to\U(d^2_n)$ be operator-norm asymptotic
representations and let $(x_n)$ be uniformly operator-norm bounded
$d^1_n\times d^2_n$ matrices with
\[
 \frac{\operatorname{Tr}\,\bigl|x_n-U^1_n(\iota(\gamma))\,x_n\,
   U^2_n(\iota(\gamma))^*\bigr|^2}{d^1_n+d^2_n}\longrightarrow0
 \qquad(\gamma\in\Gamma).
\]
Then $U^1_n(s)\,x_n\,U^2_n(s)^*$ satisfies the same convergence for
every $\gamma\in\Gamma$.
\leanverified{Sofic/ScaledKazhdanTransport}{GroupApproximation.ScaledKazhdanTransport.scaled_transport_both}
\leanverified{Sofic/IntertwinerKazhdanTransport}{GroupApproximation.IntertwinerKazhdanTransport.manuscriptIntertwinerTransport}
\leanverified{Sofic/IntertwinerKazhdanTransport}{GroupApproximation.IntertwinerKazhdanTransport.scaled_intertwiner_transport}
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(1)  Give each coordinate space with $\nu_n>0$ the inner product
$\langle x,y\rangle=\operatorname{Tr}(y^*x)/\nu_n$; an index with
$\nu_n=0$ forces $x_n=0$.  Conjugation by a unitary is unitary for every
weight, and both hypotheses and both conclusions concern only the
renormalized norms, so the ultraproduct proof of
Theorem~\ref{thm:kazhdan-transport} applies with $\tr_{d_n}$ replaced
by $\operatorname{Tr}(\,\cdot\,)/\nu_n$ and nothing else changed: the
bound $\operatorname{Tr}(x_n^*x_n)\le C\nu_n$ defines the ultraproduct
vector, the adjoint actions remain
operator-norm almost multiplicative because the weight does not enter
the operator norm, and finiteness of the norm ultraproduct turns
$P\le VPV^{*}$ into $P=VPV^{*}$, which gives both directions.

(2)  The block sum $U^{\oplus}_n=U^1_n\oplus U^2_n$ is an operator-norm
asymptotic representation, since block-diagonal operator norms are
maxima.  The off-diagonal embedding of $x_n$ has operator norm
$\opnorm{x_n}$, its commutator with $U^{\oplus}_n(\iota(\gamma))$ is the
off-diagonal embedding of the intertwining defect --- whose normalized
Hilbert--Schmidt norm on the block space has the denominator
$d^1_n+d^2_n$ --- and conjugation by $U^{\oplus}_n(s)$ acts on the embedded
corner by $x_n\mapsto U^1_n(s)\,x_n\,U^2_n(s)^*$.
Theorem~\ref{thm:kazhdan-transport} applied to $U^{\oplus}_n$ transports the
embedded sequence.  Replacing the denominator $d^1_n+d^2_n$ by an
arbitrary weight $\nu_n$ and applying (1) to the block sum instead of
Theorem~\ref{thm:kazhdan-transport} gives the intertwiner transport at
every weight.
\end{proof}

Taking $\nu_n=d_n$ gives Theorem~\ref{thm:kazhdan-transport} with the
uniform operator-norm bound relaxed to a uniform bound on
$\operatorname{Tr}(x_n^*x_n)/d_n$.  Taking $\nu_n=k_n$, the rank of
Step~3 in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse}, gives the
normalization used there.

Invariant tensors of type $(p,q)$ may be identified with Hilbert--Schmidt
intertwiners from the $q$th to the $p$th tensor power.  Tensor powers of unitaries are exactly
multiplicative and satisfy
$\opnorm{A^{\otimes p}-B^{\otimes p}}\le p\,\opnorm{A-B}$, so tensor
powers of an operator-norm asymptotic representation are again
operator-norm asymptotic representations, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:transport-variants}(2) applies to every pair of them:
at each fixed tensor type and each weight, a sequence asymptotically
invariant for the conjugate copy of the Kazhdan image is already
asymptotically invariant for the whole of it.
\leanverified{Sofic/TensorPowerTransport}{GroupApproximation.TensorPowerTransport.manuscriptFixedTensorTransport}

\section{Obstructions from a one-sided conjugation datum}\label{sec:conj}

Each result below places a subgroup of $H$ in the kernel of every
homomorphism $H\to\U(\mathcal Q)$, by producing a corner on which that
subgroup is at once $2$-norm trivial and $2$-norm far from the
identity.  The hypothesis producing the corner varies:
\begin{itemize}
\item Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion}: a \emph{finite} normal subgroup of
 $N_{\mathrm{conj}}$, the corner being the complement of its rounded
 averaging operator --- the case of the central involution of
 Section~\ref{sec:sign};
\item Theorem~\ref{thm:normal-kazhdan}: a normal
 property-\textup{(T)} subgroup of $N_{\mathrm{conj}}$, the corner
 being a spectral projection of a Kazhdan average, with neither
 finiteness, centrality nor torsion assumed;
\item Theorem~\ref{thm:abstract-nk}: the same statement with the
 conjugation datum discarded and its only consequence, $2$-norm
 triviality, taken as the hypothesis;
\item Corollary~\ref{cor:intrinsic-nk}: that statement applied to the
 subgroup $\mathfrak D(H,L)$ of \eqref{eq:intrinsicdefect}, which
 depends on $L$ alone and not on a choice of $t$ and $c$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{definition}[Kazhdan conjugation datum]\label{def:pattern}
Let $H$ be a countable group.  A \emph{Kazhdan conjugation datum} in
$H$ is a tuple $(\Lambda,\iota,t,c)$, where $\Lambda$ is a countable
group with property~\textup{(T)}, $\iota\colon\Lambda\to H$ is a group
homomorphism, not assumed injective, and $t,c\in H$,
subject to
\begin{enumerate}
  \item[(M1)] $t\,\iota(\lambda)\,t^{-1}\in\iota(\Lambda)$ for every
    $\lambda\in\Lambda$;
  \item[(M2)] $c\,\iota(\lambda)=\iota(\lambda)\,c$ for every
    $\lambda\in\Lambda$.
\end{enumerate}
Write $d:=tct^{-1}$.  The datum determines the normal closure
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptExactWrappers}{GroupApproximation.ManuscriptExactWrappers.manuscriptMarkedKazhdanPattern}
\[
  N_{\mathrm{conj}}
  \;=\;\bigl\langle\!\bigl\langle\,
  [\,d,\iota(\lambda)\,]\;:\;\lambda\in\Lambda
  \,\bigr\rangle\!\bigr\rangle_H\;\trianglelefteq\;H .
\]
\end{definition}

For a finitely generated $\Lambda$, conditions (M1)--(M2) are imposed by
finitely many relations once generating sets are fixed.  The relations make $c$ commute with $\iota(\Lambda)$ and $d$ commute with the subgroup $t\iota(\Lambda)t^{-1}$, but they do not constrain the commutators
$[d,\iota(\lambda)]$; $N_{\mathrm{conj}}$ is
their normal closure.
Every finite normal subgroup of $H$ contained in $N_{\mathrm{conj}}$,
and more generally every normal property-\textup{(T)} subgroup
contained in it, lies in the kernel of every homomorphism
$H\to\U(\mathcal Q)$ (Theorems~\ref{thm:criterion}
and~\ref{thm:normal-kazhdan}).

\begin{definition}[$2$-norm triviality]\label{def:invisible}
Let $U=(U_n)$ be an operator-norm asymptotic representation of a
countable group $H$ and let $\omega$ be an ultrafilter refining the
cofinite filter.  Write
\[
 K_2^{\omega}(U)\;=\;\bigl\{g\in H:\
 \textstyle\lim_\omega\hsnorm{U_n(g)-1}=0\bigr\},
\]
the kernel of the homomorphism induced by $U$ into the tracial
ultraproduct of the matrix blocks along $\omega$, and hence a normal
subgroup of $H$.  An element $g$ is \emph{$2$-norm trivial} if
$g\in K_2^{\omega}(U)$ for every such $U$ and every such $\omega$.
Convergence of the full sequence, $\hsnorm{U_n(g)-1}\to0$, gives
membership along every $\omega$ at once, and is how the condition is
verified below.
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptExactWrappers}{GroupApproximation.ManuscriptExactWrappers.manuscriptHSInvisibleCharacterization}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[finite-normal obstruction criterion]\label{thm:criterion}
Let $H$ be a countable group with a Kazhdan conjugation datum
$(\Lambda,\iota,t,c)$, and let $F\trianglelefteq H$ be a finite
normal subgroup with $F\subseteq N_{\mathrm{conj}}$.  Then every
homomorphism $\Theta\colon H\to\U(\mathcal Q)$ satisfies
$\Theta(f)=1$ for all $f\in F$.
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptExactWrappers}{GroupApproximation.ManuscriptExactWrappers.manuscriptFiniteNormalObstructionCriterion}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
\emph{$2$-norm triviality of $N_{\mathrm{conj}}$.}  Let
$U_n\colon H\to\U(d_n)$ be
an operator-norm asymptotic representation of $H$ --- in particular,
coordinate unitary lifts of a homomorphism to $\U(\mathcal Q)$ --- write
$U_{g,n}=U_n(g)$, and put
\[
  K_2=\{g\in H:\hsnorm{U_{g,n}-1}\longrightarrow0\}.
\]
Unitary invariance and asymptotic multiplicativity make
$K_2\trianglelefteq H$.  Since $c$ centralizes $\iota(\Lambda)$, its
lifts belong to the asymptotic commutant in normalized Hilbert--Schmidt norm of
$\iota(\Lambda)$.  By Theorem~\ref{thm:kazhdan-transport} applied to
$x_n=U_{c,n}$, the lifts of $d=tct^{-1}$ lie there as well.  Therefore
$[d,\iota(\lambda)]\in K_2$ for every $\lambda\in\Lambda$; since
$K_2$ is normal, it contains the normal closure of these commutators:
$N_{\mathrm{conj}}\subseteq K_2$, and so $F\subseteq K_2$.
Convergence of the full sequence gives triviality along every
ultrafilter refining the cofinite filter, so every element of
$N_{\mathrm{conj}}$ is $2$-norm trivial
(Definition~\ref{def:invisible}).

\emph{The coordinate averaging corner.}  Suppose a homomorphism $\Theta$ to $\U(\mathcal Q)$ is nontrivial on $F$; choose coordinate unitary
lifts $(V_{g,n})$ of $\Theta$ and form the coordinate averaging operators
\[
 p_n=\frac1{|F|}\sum_{f\in F}V_{f,n}.
\]
Because the lifts are asymptotically multiplicative and $F$ is finite,
$(p_n)$ is asymptotically self-adjoint and idempotent, and normality of
$F$ makes it asymptotically central for all of $\Theta(H)$.  The
Hermitian part of $p_n$ is asymptotically idempotent, so its spectrum
clusters at $\{0,1\}$ and rounding at $1/2$ by continuous functional
calculus yields genuine projections at operator-norm distance $o(1)$
from $p_n$; the rounded projections inherit the asymptotic centrality.
Let $q_n$ be their complements.  Nontriviality of
$\Theta$ on $F$ forces $q_n\ne0$ along infinitely many coordinates ---
otherwise the averages converge to $1$ and $\Theta|_F$ is trivial.
Retain exactly those coordinates and relabel them by $\N$.  Compress
every lift to the corner $q_nM_{d_n}(\C)q_n\cong M_{r_n}(\C)$ with
$r_n=\operatorname{rank}q_n>0$, and polar-correct the compressed
matrices: asymptotic centrality makes each compression
$q_nV_{g,n}q_n$ almost unitary in the corner, so its polar unitary is
at operator-norm distance $o(1)$ and almost-multiplicativity is preserved by
the correction.  The result is an operator-norm asymptotic representation
$(W_{g,n})$ of $H$ on the nonzero corner blocks.  Since the corner is
the complement of the rounded average, the finite average vanishes
there:
\[
 \Bigl\lVert\sum_{f\in F}W_{f,n}\Bigr\rVert\longrightarrow0
 \quad\text{in operator norm.}
 \tag{\ensuremath{\ast}}
\]

\emph{The contradiction.}  Since $F\subseteq N_{\mathrm{conj}}$ and
$(W_{g,n})$ is itself an operator-norm asymptotic representation of $H$,
the first step applies to it: every $f\in F$ satisfies
$\hsnorm{W_{f,n}-1}\to0$.  Since $F$ is finite, the maximum of these
$|F|$ null sequences is itself null, so
\[
 \sum_{f\in F}W_{f,n}\longrightarrow |F|1
 \quad\text{in normalized Hilbert--Schmidt norm},
\]
each block with its own normalized trace.  This contradicts
\((\ast)\), because operator-norm convergence dominates normalized
Hilbert--Schmidt convergence.  Hence no homomorphism to $\U(\mathcal Q)$ is
nontrivial on $F$.
\end{proof}

The proof used only that every element of $N_{\mathrm{conj}}$ is
$2$-norm trivial, so it remains valid with $N_{\mathrm{conj}}$ replaced
by any normal subgroup all of whose elements lie in $K_2^{\omega}(U)$
for every operator-norm asymptotic representation $U$ of $H$ and every
$\omega$; Theorem~\ref{thm:compression-radical} is that statement for
$\mathfrak D(H,L)$.

The central involution obstruction
(Theorem~\ref{thm:sign-criterion}) is the case $F=\{1,w\}$, where
$p_n=\tfrac12(1+V_{w,n})$ and the complementary corner gives a model in
which $w=-1$ exactly.

\subsection{An intrinsic form}

For a subgroup
$L\le H$, let $G^{+}(L)$ be generated by the elements $s$ satisfying
$sLs^{-1}\subseteq L$ --- the \emph{one-sided conjugators} of $L$ --- and put
\begin{equation}\label{eq:intrinsicdefect}
 \mathfrak D(H,L)=
 \left\langle\!\left\langle
 [gzg^{-1},\ell]:g\in G^{+}(L),\ z\in C_H(L),\ \ell\in L
 \right\rangle\!\right\rangle_H,
\end{equation}
which depends only on the pair $(H,L)$, not on a choice of datum.

\begin{corollary}[transport along all one-sided conjugators]\label{cor:generaltransport}
Let $(r_n)$ and $V_{g,n}\in\U(r_n)$ be an operator-norm asymptotic
representation of $H$, let $\Lambda$ have property~\textup{(T)}, let
$\iota\colon\Lambda\to H$ be a homomorphism, and put $L=\iota(\Lambda)$.
Suppose that $(x_n\in M_{r_n}(\C))$ is uniformly bounded in operator norm
and belongs to the asymptotic commutant in normalized Hilbert--Schmidt norm of $L$:
\[
 \hsnorm{x_n-V_{\iota(\lambda),n}x_nV_{\iota(\lambda),n}^*}
 \longrightarrow0
 \qquad(\lambda\in\Lambda).
\]
Then every $g\in G^{+}(L)$ acts in both directions on that
asymptotic commutant.  Explicitly, both
\[
 V_{g,n}x_nV_{g,n}^*
 \quad\text{and}\quad
 V_{g,n}^*x_nV_{g,n}
\]
satisfy the preceding convergence for every $\lambda\in\Lambda$.
\leanverified{Sofic/KazhdanAsymptoticCommutant}{GroupApproximation.KazhdanAsymptoticCommutant.compressionGroup_transport_both}
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
For a one-sided conjugator $s$, Theorem~\ref{thm:kazhdan-transport}
gives forward transport, and the equality $P=VPV^*$ established in its
proof gives $V^*PV=P$, hence
$V^*\operatorname{Fix}=\operatorname{Fix}$ and reverse transport.  The
elements acting in both directions form a subgroup of $H$, which
therefore contains $G^{+}(L)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[the subgroup $\mathfrak D$ and the MF residual]
\label{thm:compression-radical}
Let $H$ and $\Gamma$ be countable groups, let $\Gamma$ have
property~\textup{(T)}, let
$\iota\colon\Gamma\to H$ be a homomorphism, and put
$L=\iota(\Gamma)$.  If $F\trianglelefteq H$ is finite and
$F\le\mathfrak D(H,L)$, then every homomorphism $H\to\U(\mathcal Q)$ maps every element of $F$ to the identity.
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptKazhdanTransport}{GroupApproximation.KazhdanAsymptoticCommutant.manuscriptCompressionRadical}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $(U_{h,n})$ be coordinate lifts of an operator-norm asymptotic
representation of $H$ and put
$K_2=\{h:\hsnorm{U_{h,n}-1}\to0\}$, a normal subgroup of $H$.
If $z\in C_H(L)$, then $(U_{z,n})$ lies in the asymptotic commutant of
$L$, and Corollary~\ref{cor:generaltransport} places
$(U_{gzg^{-1},n})$ there for every $g\in G^{+}(L)$.  Hence each
generator $[gzg^{-1},\ell]$, $\ell\in L$, displayed in
\eqref{eq:intrinsicdefect} belongs to $K_2$; since
$K_2\trianglelefteq H$, it contains their normal closure:
$\mathfrak D(H,L)\le K_2$.  Every element of $\mathfrak D(H,L)$ is
therefore $2$-norm trivial in every operator-norm asymptotic
representation of $H$.  The coordinate averaging corner of
Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion} uses only that triviality, so it applies
to any finite normal $F\le\mathfrak D(H,L)$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The normal-Kazhdan form of the obstruction}

A finite group has property~\textup{(T)}, and its averaging operator is
the projection onto its fixed space.  The next theorem replaces that
operator by the spectral projection of a Kazhdan average, and assumes
neither finiteness, centrality nor torsion.

\begin{theorem}[normal-Kazhdan obstruction]\label{thm:normal-kazhdan}
Let $H$ be a countable group with a Kazhdan conjugation datum
$(\Lambda,\iota,t,c)$, and let $K\trianglelefteq H$ be a normal
subgroup with property~\textup{(T)} such that
$K\subseteq N_{\mathrm{conj}}$.  Then every homomorphism $\Theta\colon H\to\U(\mathcal Q)$ satisfies $\Theta(k)=1$ for all
$k\in K$.
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptExactWrappers}{GroupApproximation.ManuscriptExactWrappers.manuscriptNormalKazhdanObstruction}
\end{theorem}

Finite groups have property~\textup{(T)}, so $K=F$ recovers
Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion}.
\leanverified{Sofic/NormalKazhdanMFRadical}{GroupApproximation.hasKazhdanPropertyT_of_finite}
In Theorem~\ref{thm:normal-kazhdan}, $K$ may be infinite, noncentral,
and torsion-free.

\begin{proof}
The containment $N_{\mathrm{conj}}\subseteq K_2$ from the proof of
Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion} holds for any normal subgroup, so every
element of $K$ is $2$-norm trivial in every operator-norm asymptotic
representation of $H$.

Suppose a homomorphism $\Theta$ to $\U(\mathcal Q)$ is nontrivial on $K$.  Its
image $\bar H=\Theta(H)$ is countable, hence MF, and
Proposition~\ref{prop:mf-equivalences}, applied along an exhaustion of
$\bar H$, gives an operator-norm asymptotic representation
$(V_{g,n})$ in which every fixed nontrivial element eventually stays
at operator-norm distance at least $1$ from the identity.  These
models bound only operator-norm distances; at growing dimension they
give no lower bound on normalized Hilbert--Schmidt distances.  The datum and the
subgroup pass to their $\Theta$-images with normality and
property~\textup{(T)} preserved, and $\bar K=\Theta(K)$ is nontrivial
and lies in the subgroup $N_{\mathrm{conj}}$ determined by the image
datum; it suffices to
derive a contradiction for $\bar H$.

\emph{The spectral projection $q_n$.}  Fix a finite symmetric
generating Kazhdan set $S\subseteq\bar K$ with constant
$\varepsilon_0$, at each coordinate let $h_n$ be the Hermitian part of
the average of the models of $S$, and cut the spectrum of $h_n$ at
$\theta=1-\varepsilon_0^2/(4|S|)$.  Let $q_n$ be the spectral
projection of $h_n$ for $[-1,\theta]$.  On the complementary range,
where the spectrum of $h_n$ lies above $\theta$, every model of every
element of $\bar K$ is close to the identity in operator norm, by the
Kazhdan-pair displacement estimate.  If $q_n=0$ for all large $n$,
every element of $\bar K$ would satisfy $\opnorm{V_{k,n}-1}\to0$,
contradicting the operator-norm separation of any fixed nontrivial
element of $\bar K$.  So $q_n\ne0$ along an infinite set of
coordinates, which we retain.

\emph{A $2$-norm separation on the corner.}  Equip each retained corner
block $q_nM_{d_n}(\C)q_n$ with its renormalized trace.  By the
choice of $q_n$ as the spectral projection of $h_n$ for
$[-1,\theta]$, $q_nh_nq_n\le\theta q_n$, so the average over
$S$ of the real parts of the renormalized traces of the compressed
models is at most $\theta$.  Hence at every retained coordinate some
generator in $S$ has compressed model with real renormalized trace at
most $\theta$, and therefore normalized Hilbert--Schmidt distance at
least $\sqrt{2(1-\theta)}-o(1)$ from the corner identity after polar
correction; passing to a subsequence fixes a single such generator
$s_0$.

\emph{Compression.}  Normality of $\bar K$ makes $q_n$ asymptotically
commute with the ambient models: $\opnorm{[q_n,V_{g,n}]_{-}}\to0$ for
every $g$.
Because the models are only asymptotically multiplicative,
$V_{g,n}h_nV_{g,n}^{*}$ is not the Hermitian average over the
conjugate generating set $gSg^{-1}$ of $\bar K$ but converges to it in
operator norm; that set has the same Kazhdan constant.  The
displacement and concentration estimates of
Section~\ref{app:finite-stage} apply to either average
symmetrically: a vector on which one average has spectrum above
$\theta$ is almost fixed by each generator of the other set
(displacement, transported across the finitely many conjugations by
almost-multiplicativity), hence almost lies in the corresponding range
for the other average (the concentration inequality).
Two-sided almost-containment of the two subspaces implies
$\opnorm{q_n-V_{g,n}q_nV_{g,n}^{*}}\to0$, that is,
$\opnorm{[q_n,V_{g,n}]_{-}}\to0$ for every $g\in\bar H$.  Compressing
every model to the corner $q_nM_{d_n}(\C)q_n$ and polar-correcting yields an
operator-norm asymptotic representation of all of $\bar H$ on nonzero
blocks, in which $s_0$ keeps a definite normalized Hilbert--Schmidt
distance from the identity.

\emph{The contradiction.}  The corner models form an operator-norm
asymptotic representation of $\bar H$, and $\bar K$ lies in the subgroup $N_{\mathrm{conj}}$ determined by the
image datum, so $2$-norm triviality applies to them: every element of
$\bar K$ --- in particular $s_0$ --- satisfies
$\hsnorm{W_{s_0,n}-1}\to0$ on the corner, contradicting the separation
just obtained.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[obstruction from $2$-norm triviality]\label{thm:abstract-nk}
Let $H$ be a countable group and let $D\le H$ be a subgroup every
element of which is $2$-norm trivial
(Definition~\ref{def:invisible}).  Let $K\trianglelefteq H$ be a normal subgroup with
property~\textup{(T)} such that $K\le D$.  Then every homomorphism $\Theta\colon H\to\U(\mathcal Q)$ satisfies
$\Theta(k)=1$ for all $k\in K$.
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptExactWrappers}{GroupApproximation.ManuscriptExactWrappers.manuscriptAbstractNormalKazhdanObstruction}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose a homomorphism $\Theta$ to $\U(\mathcal Q)$ is nontrivial on
$K$.  The image $\bar H=\Theta(H)$ is countable and MF, and
Proposition~\ref{prop:mf-equivalences} provides an operator-norm
asymptotic representation of $\bar H$ separating every fixed nontrivial
element.  Every operator-norm asymptotic representation of $\bar H$
composes with $\Theta$ to one of $H$, so every element of $\Theta(D)$
is again $2$-norm trivial, while $\bar K=\Theta(K)\le\Theta(D)$ is
normal, Kazhdan and nontrivial.  The spectral projection $q_n$, the
$2$-norm separation at some $s_0\in\bar K$, and the compression
constructed in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:normal-kazhdan} use only
that $\bar K$ is normal, Kazhdan and nontrivial, and $2$-norm
triviality of $s_0$ on the corner contradicts that separation.
\end{proof}

Applied to the intrinsic defect, Theorem~\ref{thm:abstract-nk} gives
the companion of Theorem~\ref{thm:compression-radical} for a normal
property-\textup{(T)} subgroup:

\begin{corollary}[normal-Kazhdan form of the intrinsic residual]\label{cor:intrinsic-nk}
Let $H$ and $\Gamma$ be countable groups, let $\Gamma$ have
property~\textup{(T)}, let $\iota\colon\Gamma\to H$ be a homomorphism,
and put $L=\iota(\Gamma)$.  If $K\trianglelefteq H$ is a normal
subgroup with property~\textup{(T)} and $K\le\mathfrak D(H,L)$, then
every homomorphism $H\to\U(\mathcal Q)$ maps every
element of $K$ to the identity.
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptExactWrappers}{GroupApproximation.ManuscriptExactWrappers.manuscriptIntrinsicNormalKazhdanRadical}
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
By the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:compression-radical}, every
element of $\mathfrak D(H,L)$ is $2$-norm trivial in every
operator-norm asymptotic representation of $H$.
Theorem~\ref{thm:abstract-nk} with $D=\mathfrak D(H,L)$ concludes.
\end{proof}

The subgroup generated by \emph{all} normal property-\textup{(T)}
subgroups of $\mathfrak D(H,L)$ therefore also lies in the kernel of
every homomorphism to $\U(\mathcal Q)$.
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptExactWrappers}{GroupApproximation.ManuscriptExactWrappers.manuscriptIntrinsicNormalKazhdanPart}

\section{The finite-dimensional obstruction}\label{sec:findim}

Every finite-dimensional representation of $E$ over every field maps $w$
to the identity (Theorem~\ref{thm:B} with $H=E$).  So
finite-dimensional linear representations do not separate the points of
$E$, no finite quotient witnesses $w\neq 1$, and the Clifford quotient of
Section~\ref{sec:witness} has to be infinite.  The central involution
$w$ lies in the kernel of every homomorphism to a finite group, so $E$
is a group of \emph{Deligne type} in the terminology of
\cite[Definition~1.2]{BDL}.  Such groups were proposed there as the
natural candidate counterexamples for all three remaining approximation
problems, and $E$ is such a counterexample for the operator norm.

The same calculation proves the final, subgroup-valued assertion of
Theorem~\ref{thm:B}.  The elements of $H$ whose conjugation action
preserves $C$ in both directions form a subgroup.  The argument above
places every one-sided conjugator in that subgroup, and therefore all
of $G^{+}(\Gamma)$ in it.  Thus, for $g\in
G^{+}(\Gamma)$ and $z\in C_H(\Gamma)$, the operator
$\pi(gzg^{-1})$ still belongs to $C$ and commutes with every
$\pi(\ell)$, $\ell\in\Gamma$.  Hence every displayed generator in
\eqref{eq:intrinsicdefect} lies in $\ker\pi$; since
$\ker\pi\trianglelefteq H$, the whole normal closure
$\mathfrak D(H,\Gamma)$ does as well.

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:notRFD}
The group $E$ of Definition~\ref{def:E} admits no injective homomorphism
into $\GL(V)$ for any finite-dimensional vector space $V$ over any field.
More strongly, every finite-dimensional linear representation maps $w$ to
the identity, and every homomorphism to a finite group maps $w$ to the
identity.
\leanverified{Sofic/LiteralFiniteDimensionalObstruction}{GroupApproximation.LiteralFiniteDimensionalObstruction.manuscriptFiniteDimensionalConsequences}
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:B} with $H=E$, the image of $\mathcal B$, and the
elements $t,c,v_1$; the hypotheses hold by the defining relations
\eqref{eq:E}.  Every finite-dimensional representation maps $w$ to the
identity, and
$w\neq1$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:witness}.  For a finite target, compose
with its faithful left regular representation on the group algebra over
$\mathbb Q$.  The composite is a finite-dimensional linear
representation, so it maps $w$ to the identity.  Evaluating the resulting identity
matrix at the basis vector of the identity element returns the image
of $w$ in the finite group.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[a conjugation-invariant size forbids strict compression]\label{rem:invariantsize}
The proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:B} used one feature of finite
dimensionality: if $V\subseteq W$ and $\dim V=\dim W<\infty$, then
$V=W$.  Abstractly, let a conjugation-invariant size $\sigma$ with
values in $\mathbb N$ be defined on the subgroups of a group $Q$, and
suppose $K\le L$ with $\sigma(L)\le\sigma(K)$ implies $K=L$.  If
$gKg^{-1}\subseteq K$ then the two have equal size, so the inclusion is
an equality: in such a target no subgroup is properly conjugated into
itself.
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptExactWrappers}{GroupApproximation.ManuscriptExactWrappers.manuscriptInvariantSizePrinciple}
Cardinality on the subgroups of a finite group qualifies, which is the
finite-quotient argument of Section~\ref{sec:intro}.

The finite-dimensional case is \emph{not} that principle applied to
subgroups.  The natural candidate size, the
dimension $\dim_k\End_H(V)$ of the commutant, is order-reversing rather
than order-preserving, and it does not separate nested subgroups: $A_4<S_4$
in the standard three-dimensional representation have the same commutant
$k\cdot1$.  Theorem~\ref{thm:B} applies the principle to the commutant
instead, where the inclusion $C\subseteq uCu^{-1}$ between subspaces of
equal finite dimension is an equality \eqref{eq:pinning}; it is that
equality, and not an equality of subgroup images, that sends the defect
to the identity.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[chain conditions suffice]\label{rem:chaincondition}
Finiteness of the invariant can be weakened to a chain condition.  A
conjugation-equivariant monotone invariant whose values satisfy the
ascending or descending chain condition takes the same value on $\Gamma$
as on $t\Gamma t^{-1}$, because an order isomorphism of such a poset
fixes every point it moves weakly upward.  The fixed
subspaces of $\Gamma$ and $t\Gamma t^{-1}$ agree in every
finite-dimensional representation over every field; the same holds for
the invariants of the induced representations on
$V^{\otimes p}\otimes(V^{*})^{\otimes q}$, of which the commutant
identity \eqref{eq:pinning} is the case $\End(V)\cong V\otimes V^{*}$;
and the Zariski closures of $\sigma(\Gamma)$ and $\sigma(t\Gamma t^{-1})$
coincide, the descending chain condition for Zariski-closed subgroups of
$\GL(V)$ coming from the Hilbert basis theorem.
\leanverified{Criterion/ChainConditionCompression}{GroupApproximation.manuscriptChainConditionRigidity}
\leanverified{Criterion/TensorInvariantRigidity}{GroupApproximation.TensorInvariantRigidity.manuscriptTensorInvariantRigidity}
\leanverified{Criterion/ClosedEnvelopeCompression}{GroupApproximation.ClosedEnvelopeCompression.manuscriptEnvelopeCompressionBlind}
\leanverified{Algebra/ZariskiDescendingChain}{GroupApproximation.ZariskiClosedSubgroup.manuscriptZariskiRepresentationEnvelopeClosed}
\end{remark}

\section{The cyclic comparison}\label{sec:necessity}

% Not the lettered Theorem C: an ordinary numbered theorem.  Cite
% \ref{thm:cyclic}; `thm:C` is a legacy alias.  See the note at thm:exactfd.
\begin{theorem}[the cyclic comparison]\label{thm:cyclic}\label{thm:C}
Define the presentation
\[
 E_{\mathrm{BS}}=\left\langle\gamma_0,t,c\ \middle|\
 \begin{aligned}
  &t\gamma_0t^{-1}=\gamma_0^2,\quad c^2=1,\quad[c,\gamma_0]=1,\\
  &w_{\mathrm{BS}}^2=1,\quad
    [w_{\mathrm{BS}},\gamma_0]=[w_{\mathrm{BS}},t]
      =[w_{\mathrm{BS}},c]=1
 \end{aligned}\right\rangle,
\]
where
$w_{\mathrm{BS}}=[tct^{-1},\gamma_0(tct^{-1})\gamma_0^{-1}]$.
Then $E_{\mathrm{BS}}$ is finitely presented and $w_{\mathrm{BS}}\ne1$.
There is an explicit surjective homomorphism from $E_{\mathrm{BS}}$ onto
the subgroup of the Clifford semidirect product generated by the
displayed images, and it maps $w_{\mathrm{BS}}$ to the nontrivial
central sign.  Nevertheless every finite-dimensional representation of
$E_{\mathrm{BS}}$ over every field maps $w_{\mathrm{BS}}$ to the identity.
\leanverified{Monsters/LiteralCyclicCalibration}{GroupApproximation.LiteralCyclicCalibration.manuscriptCyclicCalibration}
\end{theorem}

Thus being sent to the identity by every exact finite-dimensional
representation does not by itself force triviality of the distinguished word.


\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:C}]
As with $w$, the relator $w_{\mathrm{BS}}^2=1$ is redundant: $tct^{-1}$
and $\gamma_0(tct^{-1})\gamma_0^{-1}$ are involutions, so centrality
forces it by \eqref{eq:involution-reversal}.  Deleting it gives a
six-relator presentation of the same group.
The stable relation gives
\(t\langle\gamma_0\rangle t^{-1}\subseteq
\langle\gamma_0\rangle\), and \(c\) centralizes this cyclic base.
The same commutant argument as in Theorem~\ref{thm:B} therefore maps
\(w_{\mathrm{BS}}\) to the identity in every finite-dimensional
representation over every field.

For nontriviality, use the affine realization of
\(\mathrm{BS}(1,2)\) and its coset action.  The base point is fixed by the
cyclic base, while the two points represented by \(t\) and \(\gamma_0t\)
are distinct.  Attach Clifford generators to these points and map \(c\) to
the generator at the base point.  All seven relators hold, and
\[
 w_{\mathrm{BS}}\longmapsto
 [c_{t\langle\gamma_0\rangle},
  c_{\gamma_0t\langle\gamma_0\rangle}]
 =-1.
\]
Thus \(w_{\mathrm{BS}}\ne1\).  Restricting the target to the subgroup
generated by the three displayed images gives the asserted surjection
onto a Clifford quotient.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Sharpness of the Kazhdan hypothesis.}
The Clifford quotient in Theorem~\ref{thm:C} is amenable,
hence MF, and its quotient map sends $w_{\mathrm{BS}}$ to a nontrivial
element.  Composing with a faithful homomorphism from that quotient to $\U(\mathcal Q)$ gives a homomorphism $\Theta\colon E_{\mathrm{BS}}\to\U(\mathcal Q)$
with $\Theta(w_{\mathrm{BS}})\ne1$, so property~\textup{(T)} cannot be
omitted from the central involution obstruction.  In the residual language of
Section~\ref{sec:consequences},
$w_{\mathrm{BS}}\notin\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(E_{\mathrm{BS}})$.
The affine group $\mathrm{BS}(1,2)\cong\mathbb Z[1/2]\rtimes\mathbb Z$ is
solvable.  The Clifford group on its coset space is locally finite, so
their semidirect product is amenable.  That quotient is a subgroup
of this amenable group and is therefore amenable, hence MF by \cite{TWW}.
Its distinguished word is the nontrivial Clifford sign by the calculation above.

The homomorphism to $\U(\mathcal Q)$ just constructed cannot be
assembled from exact finite-dimensional representations: it is an
exact homomorphism, but any coordinate lifts of it are asymptotically
multiplicative without being multiplicative.  Indeed, neither
$E_{\mathrm{BS}}$ nor its Clifford quotient is LEF, and the latter is
not residually finite.  In every finite
group the doubling relation makes the image of
$\langle\gamma_0^2\rangle$ equal to the image of
$\langle\gamma_0\rangle$; the generator therefore commutes with the
required ``halved'' element and the distinguished word maps to the identity,
so neither group is residually finite.  Any exact local embedding of a
finite set containing the defining relators and the distinguished word
satisfies the same equations, so an exact finite-dimensional
representation would already send the distinguished word to the identity:
neither group is LEF.
\leanverified{Monsters/CyclicBaseLEFObstruction}{GroupApproximation.CyclicBaseLEFObstruction.cyclicBase_exactModel_package}

\clearpage

\section{The scaling family}\label{supp:scaling}

For $m\ge2$, let $E_m$ be the same
eight-generator presentation, replacing the three relations
$tv_i t^{-1}=v_i^2$ by $tv_i t^{-1}=v_i^m$ and leaving every other
relator unchanged; thus $E_2=E$.  Write $w_m$ for the image of the same
distinguished word $w$.

\begin{corollary}[the scaling family]\label{cor:scaling-family}
For every natural number $m\ge2$, the group $E_m$ is finitely presented and
$w_m$ is a nontrivial central involution mapped to the identity by every
homomorphism $E_m\to\U(\mathcal Q)$.  Consequently $E_m$ is not MF,
and neither $\Cmax(E_m)$ nor $\Cred(E_m)$ is an MF $C^*$-algebra.
\leanverified{Sofic/ScalingFamilyEndpoint}{GroupApproximation.ScalingFamilyEndpoint.manuscriptTheoremFamily}
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Replace the affine dilation $\operatorname{diag}(2,2,2,1)$ by
$\operatorname{diag}(m,m,m,1)$ (both acting as in
Lemma~\ref{lem:linear}).  It conjugates each integral translation
$v_i$ to $v_i^m$ and fixes the three linear generators.  Its inverse sends
$v_1$ to translation by $e_1/m$, which is not integral; hence the two
Clifford generators remain distinct and $w_m$ maps to $-1$.  The Kazhdan base,
the one-sided conjugation argument, and the central involution obstruction are
independent of $m$.
\end{proof}

\section{The MF residual}\label{sec:consequences}

\begin{definition}\label{def:radical}
For a countable group $G_1$, the \emph{MF residual} is
\[
  \operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G_1)
  \;=\;\bigcap\,\bigl\{\ker\Theta:\ \Theta\ \text{a homomorphism }G_1\to\U(\mathcal Q)\bigr\},
\]
a normal subgroup of $G_1$.  If $G_1/N$ is MF then composing
$G_1\to G_1/N$ with an injective homomorphism $G_1/N\to\U(\mathcal Q)$
gives a homomorphism with kernel $N$, so
$\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G_1)\le N$: it is the smallest normal
subgroup with MF quotient, which is the sense in which \emph{residual}
is used here and for the nilpotent and finite residuals.  The
intersection does not change if the
dimension sequences are allowed to be arbitrary positive rather than
strictly increasing (Proposition~\ref{prop:mf-equivalences}).  This is the
operator-norm analogue of
the invisible subgroups of \cite[Definitions~2.5--2.7]{SlofstraCorrelations}
and of the metric-ultraproduct approximation framework of \cite{NST}.
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptExactWrappers}{GroupApproximation.ManuscriptExactWrappers.manuscriptMFRadical}
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[functoriality]\label{lem:portable}
For every homomorphism $\varphi\colon G_1\to G_2$ of countable
groups,
\[
  \varphi\bigl(\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G_1)\bigr)
  \;\subseteq\;\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G_2):
\]
the MF residual is functorial.  In particular, if
$\varphi\colon E\to G_2$ satisfies $\varphi(w)\neq1$, then $G_2$ is
not MF.
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptExactWrappers}{GroupApproximation.ManuscriptExactWrappers.manuscriptRadicalPortability}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $x\in\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G_1)$ and $\Theta$ is a
homomorphism $G_2\to\U(\mathcal Q)$, then $\Theta\circ\varphi$ is a homomorphism $G_1\to\U(\mathcal Q)$, so $\Theta(\varphi(x))=1$.  For the second
statement, $w\in\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(E)$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:A}, so
$1\neq\varphi(w)\in\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G_2)$, and an
injective homomorphism $G_2\to\U(\mathcal Q)$ would map $\varphi(w)$ to $1$, a
contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[the universal MF quotient]\label{prop:univquot}
Let $G_1$ be a countable group and
$R=\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G_1)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $R$ is the kernel of a single homomorphism $G_1\to\U(\mathcal Q)$.
\item $G_1/R$ is MF.
\item Every homomorphism from $G_1$ to an MF group factors through
$G_1/R$.
\end{enumerate}
In particular $G_1$ is MF if and only if $R=\{1\}$.
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptExactWrappers}{GroupApproximation.ManuscriptExactWrappers.manuscriptUniversalMFQuotient}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
(3) If $\varphi\colon G_1\to M$ with $M$ MF and
$j\colon M\to\U(\mathcal Q((d_n)))$ is an injective homomorphism to $\U(\mathcal Q)$,
then $j\circ\varphi$ is a homomorphism $G_1\to\U(\mathcal Q)$, so
$j(\varphi(R))=1$ and, by injectivity of $j$, $\varphi(R)=1$.

(1) and (2).  Work on $Q=G_1/R$.  Every homomorphism $G_1\to\U(\mathcal Q)$ maps $R$ to $1$, and for each $g\notin R$ there is one with
$\Theta(g)\ne1$, which descends; so for every $\bar g\ne1$ in $Q$ there
is a homomorphism $\Theta_{\bar g}\colon Q\to\U(\mathcal Q)$ with
$\Theta_{\bar g}(\bar g)\ne1$.  Enumerate the nonidentity elements and
these representations, pass to coordinate unitary lifts, and
diagonalize: at stage
$k$, select in each of the first $k$ of them a coordinate at which
multiplicativity holds within $1/k$ on the first $k$ group elements
and each of the first $k$ designated elements stays separated by its fixed
positive constant --- possible because each $\Theta_{\bar g}$ separates
its own element along infinitely many coordinates --- and take the
block sum.  Block operator norms are maxima, so the resulting sequence
is an operator-norm asymptotic representation separating every nonidentity element from
the identity; its class,
composed with the polar-correction isomorphism of
Lemma~\ref{lem:unitarycorona}, is a faithful homomorphism $Q\to\U(\mathcal Q)$.  This proves (2), and composing with
$G_1\twoheadrightarrow Q$ proves (1).  Finally, if $G_1$ is MF then an
injective homomorphism to $\U(\mathcal Q)$ gives $R=\{1\}$; if $R=\{1\}$ then
(2) says $G_1$ is MF.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[exact residual from a candidate quotient]\label{cor:exactradical}
Let $N\trianglelefteq G_1$ satisfy
$N\le\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G_1)$.  Every homomorphism $G_1\to\U(\mathcal Q)$ factors uniquely through $G_1/N$.  If in
addition $G_1/N$ is MF, then
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptExactWrappers}{GroupApproximation.ManuscriptExactWrappers.manuscriptExactRadicalFromCandidateQuotient}
\[
 \operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G_1)=N.
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The containment $N\le\ker\Theta$ gives the unique quotient
factorization for every such $\Theta$.  For the
reverse containment in the displayed equality, compose the quotient
map $G_1\to G_1/N$ with an injective homomorphism $G_1/N\to\U(\mathcal Q)$:
its kernel is exactly $N$, while the MF residual lies in the kernel of
every homomorphism to $\U(\mathcal Q)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[residual reduction to the quotient]\label{cor:pullback}
Let $N\trianglelefteq G_1$ satisfy
$N\le\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G_1)$ and let
$q\colon G_1\to G_1/N$ be the quotient map.  Then
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptExactWrappers}{GroupApproximation.ManuscriptExactWrappers.manuscriptRadicalReductionToQuotient}
\[
 \operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G_1)
 \;=\;
 q^{-1}\bigl(\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G_1/N)\bigr).
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
For the forward containment, Lemma~\ref{lem:portable} gives
$q(\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G_1))
\subseteq\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G_1/N)$.  Conversely, suppose
$q(x)\in\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G_1/N)$ and let $\Theta$ be a
homomorphism $G_1\to\U(\mathcal Q)$.  Every homomorphism $G_1\to\U(\mathcal Q)$
maps $N$ to $1$ by hypothesis, so $\Theta$ factors as $\bar\Theta\circ q$
with $\bar\Theta$ a homomorphism $G_1/N\to\U(\mathcal Q)$; hence
$\Theta(x)=\bar\Theta(q(x))=1$.
\end{proof}

By Corollary~\ref{cor:pullback}, the passage from $N$ to the full
preimage of the quotient residual is a closure operation on the normal
subgroups of a fixed group.

\begin{proposition}[the MF closure of relations]\label{prop:mfclosure}
For a countable group $G_1$ and $N\trianglelefteq G_1$ with quotient map
$q\colon G_1\to G_1/N$, put
\[
 \operatorname{cl}_{\mathrm{MF}}(N)
 =q^{-1}\bigl(\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G_1/N)\bigr).
\]
An element belongs to $\operatorname{cl}_{\mathrm{MF}}(N)$ exactly when
every homomorphism $G_1\to\U(\mathcal Q)$ that maps $N$ to the identity maps
it to the identity.
\leanverified{Sofic/MFRelationClosure}{GroupApproximation.mem_actualCoronaMFClosure_iff}
The operation $N\mapsto\operatorname{cl}_{\mathrm{MF}}(N)$ is extensive,
monotone, and idempotent on normal subgroups;
$G_1/\operatorname{cl}_{\mathrm{MF}}(N)$ is MF; and
$\operatorname{cl}_{\mathrm{MF}}(N)=N$ exactly when $G_1/N$ is MF.
\leanverified{Sofic/MFRelationClosure}{GroupApproximation.manuscriptRelationClosure}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Homomorphisms $G_1\to\U(\mathcal Q)$ that map $N$ to $1$ correspond to homomorphisms $G_1/N\to\U(\mathcal Q)$, which gives the membership
characterization; extensivity, monotonicity, and idempotence are read
off from it.  The quotient clause is Proposition~\ref{prop:univquot}(2)
transported along the third isomorphism theorem, and the fixed-point
clause is the final equivalence of Proposition~\ref{prop:univquot}.
\end{proof}

For a presentation $G=\langle X\mid R\rangle=F/N$, the closure of $N$
consists of the relations satisfied by every solution of $R$ in every
MF group, and the group is MF exactly when $N$ is closed.
The distinguished word of Theorem~\ref{thm:A} lies in
$\operatorname{cl}_{\mathrm{MF}}(N)\setminus N$ for the forty-one
relators (Proposition~\ref{prop:horn}).

\begin{proposition}[iterating closure and join]\label{prop:sounditeration}
Let $\mathcal S$ be the smallest family of subgroups of $G_1$ that
contains the trivial subgroup and is closed under the following two
operations: from a \emph{normal} $N\in\mathcal S$, adjoin every
subgroup of $\operatorname{cl}_{\mathrm{MF}}(N)$; and from any
subfamily of $\mathcal S$, adjoin its join, the subgroup generated by
the union.  Then every member of $\mathcal S$ is contained in
$\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G_1)$; a normal member with MF
quotient equals $\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G_1)$; and if
$G_1\in\mathcal S$, every homomorphism from $G_1$ to a countable MF
group is trivial.
\leanverified{Sofic/MFRelationClosure}{GroupApproximation.manuscriptSoundIterationClosure}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Induct over the two operations.  If a normal $N\in\mathcal S$
satisfies $N\le\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G_1)$, then
Corollary~\ref{cor:pullback} gives
$\operatorname{cl}_{\mathrm{MF}}(N)=\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G_1)$,
so every subgroup chosen in the first operation is again inside the
residual --- normal or not.  For the second operation, the residual is
itself a subgroup, hence an upper bound in the subgroup lattice for any
family contained in it; so the join $\bigvee_iH_i=\langle\bigcup_iH_i\rangle$
lies in the residual too.  The remaining assertions are
Corollary~\ref{cor:exactradical} and Proposition~\ref{prop:univquot};
the latter needs $N$ normal so that $G_1/N$ is a group.
\end{proof}

Each of Theorems~\ref{thm:criterion}, \ref{thm:normal-kazhdan},
and~\ref{thm:collapse} puts a subgroup inside the residual of every
countable group carrying its data, hence inside the residual of every
quotient reached by the iteration.  A hypothesis may hold at a later
stage without holding at an earlier one: modulo the earlier stage an
element may acquire finite order, an orbit may begin to commute, or a
subgroup may become normal.

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:nofaithful}
Let $A$ be a unital $C^*$-algebra admitting an injective, possibly
nonunital, $*$-homomorphism into a matrix quotient.  Then the group $E$
admits no injective homomorphism into $\U(A)$.
\leanverified{Sofic/LiteralNonMFConsequences}{GroupApproximation.LiteralNonMFConsequences.literal_no_faithful_corona_subalgebra_target}
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Let $j\colon A\to\mathcal Q$ be the possibly nonunital inclusion into the
ambient matrix quotient and let $\phi\colon E\to\U(A)$.  Complement correction gives
\[
   \widehat j(u)=j(u)+1-j(1_A)\in\U(\mathcal Q).
\]
This is an injective homomorphism on \(\U(A)\), so
\(\widehat j\circ\phi\) is a homomorphism \(E\to\U(\mathcal Q)\).
By Theorem~\ref{thm:A}, \((\widehat j\circ\phi)(w)=1\); injectivity
of \(\widehat j\) gives \(\phi(w)=1\).  Proposition~\ref{prop:witness} has
\(w\neq1\), so \(\phi\) cannot be injective.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Permanence and its failure}

\begin{lemma}[permanence]\label{lem:permanence}
Let all groups be countable.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Every subgroup of an MF group is MF.
\item Every residually finite group is MF.
\item Every locally finite group is MF.
\leanverified{Sofic/OperatorMFPositiveControls}{GroupApproximation.IsOperatorMF.subgroup}
\leanverified{Sofic/OperatorMFPositiveControls}{GroupApproximation.isOperatorMF_of_residuallyFinite}
\leanverified{Sofic/LocallyFiniteMF}{GroupApproximation.isOperatorMF_of_locallyFinite}
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
(1)  Restrict an injective homomorphism to $\U(\mathcal Q)$.  (2)  For a finite
test set $F$, a finite quotient injective on $F$, composed with its
left regular permutation action, is an exact finite-dimensional representation; distinct
permutations differ in some column of a permutation matrix, giving
operator-norm separation at least $1$, and the local-to-global
direction of Proposition~\ref{prop:mf-equivalences} concludes.
(3)  A finite test set lies in a finite subgroup $K$.  The left
regular representation $\lambda_K\colon K\to\U(\C^{K})$ is a
finite-dimensional unitary representation of $K$ by permutation
matrices, and the local model that sends each test element $g$ to
$\lambda_K(g)$ is exactly multiplicative on the test set, since a
product of test elements again lies in $K$.  It separates as in (2).
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[quotients]\label{cor:quotclosure}
MF groups are not closed under quotients: the free group
\(F_8\) is MF, and the explicit group \(E\) is its quotient
but is not MF.
\leanverified{Sofic/LiteralMFQuotientControls}{GroupApproximation.LiteralMFQuotientControls.manuscriptQuotientNonclosure}
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Map the eight free generators onto the eight displayed generators of \(E\).
The resulting homomorphism is surjective because those generators define the
displayed presentation.  The group \(F_8\) is residually finite, hence MF by
Lemma~\ref{lem:permanence}(2), while \(E\) is not MF by Theorem~\ref{thm:A}.
\end{proof}


\subsection{Matricial stability and the residual}\label{subsec:stability}

Following Dadarlat \cite{Dadarlat}, a countable group $G$ is
\emph{matricially stable} if for every operator-norm asymptotic
representation $(V_n)$ of $G$ there are genuine finite-dimensional
unitary representations $\pi_n$, in the same matrix dimensions, with
$\opnorm{V_n(g)-\pi_n(g)}\to0$ for every $g\in G$.
Write $\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{fd}}(G)$ for the intersection of
the kernels of all finite-dimensional unitary representations of $G$.

\begin{proposition}[stability identifies the residuals]\label{prop:stabradical}
For every countable group,
$\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G)\le\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{fd}}(G)$.
If $G$ is matricially stable, then
$\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G)=\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{fd}}(G)$;
in particular a stable group with
$\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{fd}}(G)\neq1$ is not MF.
\leanverified{Sofic/MatricialStabilityRadical}{GroupApproximation.MatricialStabilityRadical.actualCoronaMFResidual_le_fdUnitaryResidual}
\leanverified{Sofic/MatricialStabilityRadical}{GroupApproximation.MatricialStabilityRadical.actualCoronaMFResidual_eq_fdUnitaryResidual}
\leanverified{Sofic/MatricialStabilityRadical}{GroupApproximation.MatricialStabilityRadical.not_isCDEOperatorMF_of_stable_of_fdResidual_ne_bot}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The first inclusion holds for every countable group.  A
finite-dimensional unitary
representation $\pi$ on $\C^{d}$ amplifies to $\pi^{\oplus n}$ on
$\C^{nd}$; the dimensions $nd$ are strictly increasing, so
$(\pi^{\oplus n})_n$ is a homomorphism to $\U(\mathcal Q)$ in the sense of
\eqref{eq:unitary-corona}.  Since
$\opnorm{\pi^{\oplus n}(x)-1}=\opnorm{\pi(x)-1}$ for every $n$, that
sequence is null exactly when $\pi(x)=1$.  Hence an element of
$\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(G)$ is sent to the identity by every finite-dimensional
representation.

For the converse inclusion, assume $G$ matricially stable,
let $x\in\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{fd}}(G)$, and suppose toward a
contradiction that a homomorphism $\Theta$ to $\U(\mathcal Q)$ has
$\Theta(x)\ne1$.  Choose unitary coordinate lifts $U_n(g)$ of
$\Theta(g)$.  Since $\Theta(x)-1\ne0$ in the norm quotient, after
passing to a subsequence there is $\delta>0$ with
$\opnorm{U_n(x)-1}\ge\delta$ for every $n$.  The lifts form an
operator-norm asymptotic representation, so stability gives genuine
representations $\pi_n$ on the same blocks with
$\opnorm{U_n(g)-\pi_n(g)}\to0$ for each fixed $g$.  But
$x\in\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{fd}}(G)$ gives $\pi_n(x)=1$ for every
$n$, so
$\opnorm{U_n(x)-1}=\opnorm{U_n(x)-\pi_n(x)}\to0$, against the uniform
lower bound.
\end{proof}

For a stable group the two residuals therefore agree.  With
Theorem~\ref{thm:B}: every matricially stable group with
$\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{fd}}(G)\ne1$ is not MF.

\section{Orbit collapse}\label{subsec:collapse}

Under a
commuting-orbit hypothesis in place of centrality, every homomorphism
to $\U(\mathcal Q)$ identifies an entire conjugation orbit of an
involution; a whole family of commutators therefore lies in the
residual, and by Corollary~\ref{cor:pullback} computing the residual
reduces to the quotient by that family.

\begin{definition}[involutive orbit witness]\label{def:invwitness}
Let $H$ be a group, $L\le H$ a subgroup, and $s\in H$.  An element
$k\in H$ is an \emph{involutive orbit witness} for $(L,s)$ if
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(W1)] $k^2=1$;
\item[(W2)] $s\gamma s^{-1}$ commutes with $k$ for every $\gamma\in L$;
\item[(W3)] the conjugates $\gamma_1k\gamma_1^{-1}$ and
  $\gamma_2k\gamma_2^{-1}$ commute for all $\gamma_1,\gamma_2\in L$.
\end{enumerate}
The pair $(L,s)$ determines the normal subgroup
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptExactWrappers}{GroupApproximation.ManuscriptExactWrappers.manuscriptInvolutiveCollapsePattern}
\[
  D_{\mathrm{coll}}(L,s)
  \;=\;
  \bigl\langle\!\bigl\langle\,
  [\gamma,k]\;:\;\gamma\in L,\
  k\text{ a witness for }(L,s)
  \,\bigr\rangle\!\bigr\rangle_H .
\]
\end{definition}

In a permutational wreath product $K^{(X)}\rtimes G$ with $L\le G$
conjugated into itself by $s$ and $x_0\in X$ a point fixed by
$sLs^{-1}$, every
involution of the copy of $K$ at $x_0$ is a witness: copies at distinct
points commute, and the conjugate copy fixes the point.  Every
homomorphism to $\U(\mathcal Q)$ then identifies those copies along the
whole $L$-orbit of $x_0$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse}).

\begin{theorem}[involutive collapse]\label{thm:collapse}
Let $H$ be countable, let $L\le H$ be a subgroup with
property~\textup{(T)}, and let $s\in H$ satisfy
$s\gamma s^{-1}\in L$ for every $\gamma\in L$.  Then
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptExactWrappers}{GroupApproximation.ManuscriptExactWrappers.manuscriptInvolutiveCollapse}
\[
  D_{\mathrm{coll}}(L,s)\;\le\;\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(H).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix a witness $k$ and suppose, toward a contradiction, that some homomorphism $\Theta\colon H\to\U(\mathcal Q)$ has
$\Theta([\gamma_0,k])\ne1$ for some
$\gamma_0\in L$.
Coordinate unitary lifts (Lemma~\ref{lem:unitarycorona}) give an
operator-norm asymptotic representation $(U_{g,n})$ on blocks
$M_{d_n}(\C)$, and after passing to a subsequence there is $\eta>0$
with $\opnorm{U_{[\gamma_0,k],n}-1}\ge\eta$ for every $n$.

\emph{Step 1: approximation by commuting self-adjoint involutions.}
Write $v_\gamma=\gamma k\gamma^{-1}$ and let
$O=\{v_\gamma:\gamma\in L\}$ be the conjugation orbit of $k$; by (W1),
(W3), and asymptotic multiplicativity the models of the elements of
$O$ asymptotically commute and square to $1$.  The spectral sign of
the Hermitian part of a unitary $u$ is an exact self-adjoint
involution at operator distance at most $\opnorm{u^2-1}$ from $u$.
Enumerate $O$ as $v^{(1)},v^{(2)},\dots$ and correct in that order,
carrying the induction hypothesis that after stage $m$ the operators
$V_n(v^{(1)}),\dots,V_n(v^{(m)})$ are exact self-adjoint involutions
commuting pairwise, \emph{and that no later stage alters them}.  At
stage $m$ the model $U_{v^{(m)},n}$ fails to commute with $V_n(v^{(j)})$, $j<m$, by at most
$\opnorm{[U_{v^{(m)},n},U_{v^{(j)},n}]_{-}}
 +2\opnorm{V_n(v^{(j)})-U_{v^{(j)},n}}$,
the first term an asymptotic-commutation error of (W3) and the second
the error already accumulated, so the total is a sum of $m$ quantities
each tending to $0$.  The joint commutant of
$V_n(v^{(1)}),\dots,V_n(v^{(m-1)})$ is a unital $*$-subalgebra --- a
finite intersection of joint spectral corners of exact commuting
involutions --- so compressing $U_{v^{(m)},n}$ into it moves it by at
most that amount, and taking the spectral sign of the Hermitian part
\emph{inside that subalgebra} yields an exact self-adjoint involution
commuting exactly with all earlier ones while leaving them untouched.
The resulting perturbation is bounded by the sum of the preceding $m$
commutation and correction errors, hence tends to $0$ for fixed $m$;
each $m$ is fixed before $n$ grows, so the limits displayed next are
taken at fixed $m$.
The induction yields exact pairwise-commuting self-adjoint involutions
$V_n(v)$, $v\in O$, with
\[
 \opnorm{V_n(v)-U_{v,n}}\longrightarrow0,\qquad
 \opnorm{U_{g,n}V_n(v)U_{g,n}^{*}-V_n(gvg^{-1})}\longrightarrow0
\]
for each fixed $v\in O$ and $g\in L$.  Write $V_n(\gamma)$ for
$V_n(v_\gamma)$; it depends on $\gamma$ only through $v_\gamma$.

\emph{Step 2: a rank pseudometric on the orbit.}  For commuting exact
involutions $e,f$ the self-adjoint displacement $T=e-f$ satisfies
$T^3=4T$, so its eigenvalues lie in $\{0,\pm2\}$, the operator $T^2/4$
is the orthogonal projection onto $\operatorname{ran}T$, and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rank-support}
 \operatorname{Tr}|e-f|^2=4\operatorname{rank}(e-f).
\end{equation}
Hence $r_n(\gamma,\mu)=\operatorname{rank}(V_n(\gamma)-V_n(\mu))$
is an integer-valued pseudometric.  It is eventually translation
invariant, $r_n(g\gamma,g\mu)=r_n(\gamma,\mu)$ for all large $n$ and
each fixed triple: the comparison is made between the projections
$T^2/4$, whose ranks agree once they lie at operator distance below
$1$, and the displacements have operator norm at most $2$, so a
covariance error below $1/8$ moves the associated projections by at
most $1/4$.

\emph{Step 3: normalization at the generators.}  Property~\textup{(T)} makes
$L$ finitely generated; fix a Kazhdan pair $(S,\kappa)$ with $S$
finite symmetric and put $k_n=\sum_{a\in S}r_n(1,a)$.  For each fixed
$\gamma$, translation invariance and the triangle inequality along one
fixed word for $\gamma$ give $r_n(1,\gamma)\le|\gamma|_S\,k_n$ for all
large $n$, where $|\cdot|_S$ is the word length.  Suppose $k_n=0$
along a subsequence.  Only $\gamma_0$ is needed there, and
the estimate above is available for it: $r_n(1,\gamma_0)\le
|\gamma_0|_S\,k_n=0$ at every sufficiently late stage of that
subsequence, so $V_n(\gamma_0)=V_n(1)$ and
$\opnorm{U_{[\gamma_0,k],n}-1}\to0$ along it, against the separation.
Hence $k_n\ge1$ for all large $n$.  (The bound $r_n(1,\gamma)\le
|\gamma|_S k_n$ holds for each fixed $\gamma$ past a stage depending on
$\gamma$, so it does not collapse the whole orbit at one stage when $L$
is infinite; the argument does not ask it to.)

\emph{Step 4: an approximate $1$-cocycle.}  This step and the next work
with the \emph{unnormalized} Hilbert--Schmidt, or Frobenius, norm
$\|x\|_{\mathrm F}=\operatorname{Tr}(x^{*}x)^{1/2}$, so that
$\|x\|_{\mathrm F}^2=d_n\hsnorm{x}^2$ on $M_{d_n}(\C)$; the
normalization is by the rank $k_n$ below rather than by $d_n$.  Set
$b_n(\gamma)=k_n^{-1/2}\bigl(V_n(\gamma)-V_n(1)\bigr)$.  By
\eqref{eq:rank-support} and Steps 2 and 3,
$\|b_n(\gamma)\|_{\mathrm{F}}^2=4r_n(1,\gamma)/k_n\le4\,|\gamma|_S$ at
every large stage, and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:generator-mass}
  \sum_{a\in S}\|b_n(a)\|_{\mathrm{F}}^2=4
\end{equation}
whenever $k_n\ge1$.  Conjugation by $U_{a,n}$ is a Frobenius isometry.
Fix $a,\gamma\in L$, put $\mu=a^{-1}\gamma$, and let $k_n^{-1/2}D_n$ be
the defect in the cocycle identity, so that
\[
  D_n=\bigl(V_n(\gamma)-V_n(1)\bigr)
  -U_{a,n}\bigl(V_n(\mu)-V_n(1)\bigr)U_{a,n}^{*}
  -\bigl(V_n(a)-V_n(1)\bigr).
\]
As written, $D_n$ is a sum of three displacements of the commuting
family of Step~1, one of them conjugated; rank is subadditive and
invariant under unitary conjugation, so Step~2 with the bound of
Step~3 gives
\[
  \operatorname{rank}D_n\le r_n(\gamma,1)+r_n(\mu,1)+r_n(a,1)
  \le\bigl(|\gamma|_S+|\mu|_S+|a|_S\bigr)k_n
\]
eventually.  Regrouped as
\[
  D_n=\bigl(V_n(\gamma)-U_{a,n}V_n(\mu)U_{a,n}^{*}\bigr)
  +\bigl(U_{a,n}V_n(1)U_{a,n}^{*}-V_n(a)\bigr),
\]
the same $D_n$ is a sum of two covariance errors of Step~1, so
$\opnorm{D_n}\to0$.  Both bounds are needed, because operator-norm
control alone does not bound rank: two exact involutions at small
operator distance can differ in full rank.  Combining them in
$\operatorname{Tr}|D|^2\le\operatorname{rank}(D)\cdot\opnorm{D}^2$
gives
$\operatorname{Tr}|D_n|^{2}/k_n
\le\bigl(|\gamma|_S+|\mu|_S+|a|_S\bigr)\opnorm{D_n}^2\to0$: the
cocycle defect is $o(k_n)$ in squared Frobenius norm.

\emph{Step 5: the limiting cocycle is a coboundary.}  Fix a free
ultrafilter $\omega$ on $\N$, give $M_{d_n}(\C)$ the inner product
$\langle x,y\rangle=\operatorname{Tr}(y^{*}x)$, and let $K_\omega$ be
the Hilbert-space ultraproduct of these spaces along $\omega$.
Conjugation is unitary for that inner product, and the dimension-free
estimate $\opnorm{\Ad u_1-\Ad u_2}\le2\opnorm{u_1-u_2}$ makes
$g\mapsto\Ad U_{g,n}$ asymptotically multiplicative in the operator
norm of $B(M_{d_n}(\C))$, so
\[
 \pi(g)=[\Ad U_{g,n}]_\omega
\]
is a unitary representation of $H$ on $K_\omega$.  The bound
$\|b_n(\gamma)\|_{\mathrm F}^2\le4|\gamma|_S$ of Step~4 makes
$b(\gamma)=[b_n(\gamma)]_\omega$ a well-defined vector of $K_\omega$
for every $\gamma\in L$, and the vanishing of the normalized cocycle
defect makes
\[
 b(a\mu)=b(a)+\pi(a)\,b(\mu)\qquad(a,\mu\in L),
\]
so $b\colon L\to K_\omega$ is a $1$-cocycle for $\pi|_L$.  Since
$\lim_\omega$ is linear, \eqref{eq:generator-mass} passes to the limit
as the identity
\begin{equation}\label{eq:limit-mass}
 \sum_{a\in S}\lVert b(a)\rVert^{2}=4 .
\end{equation}
By the Delorme--Guichardet theorem \cite[Theorem~2.12.4]{BHV},
property~\textup{(T)} of $L$ makes every $1$-cocycle of every unitary
representation a coboundary, so there is $y\in K_\omega$ with
\[
 b(\gamma)=y-\pi(\gamma)y\qquad(\gamma\in L).
\]

\emph{Step 6: transport by the conjugator $s$.}  By (W2) the element
$sas^{-1}$ commutes with $k$ for every $a\in L$, so
$v_{sas^{-1}}=k=v_1$; the assignment $\gamma\mapsto v_\gamma$ of Step~1 then gives
$V_n(sas^{-1})=V_n(1)$ at every stage, and hence $b(sas^{-1})=0$.
Therefore $y=\pi(sas^{-1})y$ for every $a\in L$: the vector $y$ is
fixed by $\pi(sLs^{-1})$.

The proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:kazhdan-transport} gives the reverse of
the inclusion supplied by $sLs^{-1}\le L$, namely
\[
 \operatorname{Fix}\pi(sLs^{-1})\;\subseteq\;\operatorname{Fix}\pi(L):
\]
there $P$ is the projection of $K_\omega$ onto $\operatorname{Fix}\pi(L)$,
the range of $Q=\pi(s)P\pi(s)^{*}$ is $\operatorname{Fix}\pi(sLs^{-1})$,
$P\le Q$, and $Q=P$ by Lemmas~\ref{lem:finitecompare}
and~\ref{lem:ultrafinite}.  That chain is independent of $(x_n)$,
and multiplying each coordinate inner product by a positive scalar ---
here passing from $\tr_{d_n}$ to $\operatorname{Tr}$ --- changes neither
the operator norms of the adjoint actions nor the comparison of
projections, so Lemma~\ref{lem:ultrafinite} applies unchanged.

Hence $y\in\operatorname{Fix}\pi(L)$, so $b(a)=y-\pi(a)y=0$ for every
$a\in L$, contradicting \eqref{eq:limit-mass}.  Therefore
$\Theta([\gamma,k])=1$ for every $\gamma\in L$ and every witness $k$,
and normality of these kernels extends the conclusion to all of
$D_{\mathrm{coll}}(L,s)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[the quantitative form]\label{rem:collapse-finite-stage}
Steps~5 and~6 can be carried out at a single coordinate, with every
tolerance fixed in advance, except for the constant $C$ below, which is
read off the limiting cocycle.  By property~\textup{(T)}, that
\emph{limiting} cocycle satisfies a bound uniform in $\gamma$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:profile-bounded}
 \lVert b(\gamma)\rVert\le C\qquad(\gamma\in L),
\end{equation}
with $C$ depending on the Kazhdan pair alone.  Step~4 gives only the
$\gamma$-dependent bound $2|\gamma|_S^{1/2}$;
\eqref{eq:profile-bounded} strengthens it to a bound uniform in
$\gamma$.

The finite-stage reading of \eqref{eq:profile-bounded} fails, and the
argument below does not use it.
For each fixed $\gamma$ the finite-stage norms $\lVert
b_n(\gamma)\rVert_{\mathrm F}$ approach $\lVert b(\gamma)\rVert$ along
$\omega$, so each $\gamma$ has its own $\omega$-large set of good
stages; there is no single stage at which the bound holds
simultaneously for all $\gamma\in L$, since $L$ is infinite and an
infinite intersection of $\omega$-large sets need not be $\omega$-large.
The bound is used at finitely many places only --- on the generators
$a\in S$ and on the primitive --- and there the intersection is finite.
Property~\textup{(T)} supplies that uniformity, in the quantitative
form of the Delorme--Guichardet argument \cite[Section~2.12]{BHV}: for
a Kazhdan pair $(S,\kappa)$, every $1$-cocycle of every unitary
representation of $L$ is bounded by its generator values, the fixed
vector produced from the Kazhdan projection lying within a multiple of
$\max_{a\in S}\lVert b(a)\rVert$ of the origin and
$b(\gamma)=y-\pi(\gamma)y$ being at most twice that.  Applied to the
limiting cocycle of Step~5, where $\sum_{a\in S}\lVert b(a)\rVert^{2}=4$
by \eqref{eq:generator-mass}, this gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:profile-explicit}
 \lVert b(\gamma)\rVert^{2}\;\le\;\frac{160\log2}{\kappa^{2}}
 \;\le\;\frac{111}{\kappa^{2}}\qquad(\gamma\in L),
\end{equation}
so $C=\sqrt{111}/\kappa$ serves.  The Kazhdan constant is the only
datum that enters: neither $|S|$ nor the group appears, which is more
than uniformity in $\gamma$ and more than
\eqref{eq:profile-bounded} asks for.
\leanverified{Sofic/CollapseProfileBound}{GroupApproximation.CollapseProfileBound.collapse_profile_le}
\leanverified{Sofic/CollapseProfileBoundNumeric}{GroupApproximation.CollapseProfileBound.collapse_profile_le_num}
That reading is the one place where the argument below
still consults the limit rather than a single stage: what is
ultraproduct-free below is the transport and the contradiction, not the
provenance of $C$.  Put
\[
 \rho=\frac1{\sqrt{|S|}+1},\qquad
 q=\frac{\rho}{24(C+1)},\qquad
 \tau\le\min\Bigl\{\frac\rho2,\;
   \frac{(1-\theta)\rho^{2}}{256(C+1)}\Bigr\},
\]
with
\[
 1-\frac{\kappa^{2}}{4|S|}<\theta<1
\]
the spectral threshold, and fix a single stage $n$, past the finitely
many thresholds of Steps~1--4, at which the covariance errors of
Step~1 are below $\tau$.  Both inequalities on $\theta$ are used.  The
upper one keeps the corner nonempty.  The lower one holds because the
Kazhdan gap of Step~4 bounds the Hermitian average by
$1-\kappa^{2}/(4|S|)$ off the fixed subspace, and it yields
\eqref{eq:corner-fix}.

The operator to cut is not quite the average.  At a finite stage
$|S|^{-1}\sum_{a\in S}\Ad U_{a,n}$ need not be self-adjoint even for
symmetric $S$, because $U_{a^{-1},n}$ is only \emph{approximately}
$U_{a,n}^{*}$; an ordered spectral cut ``above $\theta$'' is therefore
undefined for it as it stands.  Let $P_n$ be instead the spectral
projection above $\theta$ of the Hermitian part
\[
 \frac1{2|S|}\sum_{a\in S}
   \bigl(\Ad U_{a,n}+(\Ad U_{a,n})^{*}\bigr)
\]
--- the \emph{Kazhdan corner} --- and let $\widehat b_n$ be the
compression into it of the primitive supplied below.
\begin{align}
 \opnorm{(\Ad U_{a,n}-1)P_n}&\le q\qquad(a\in S),
 \label{eq:corner-fix}\\
 \opnorm{(1-P_n)\,Q_n}&\le q,\qquad
 Q_n=\Ad U_{s,n}\,P_n\,\Ad U_{s,n}^{*} .
 \label{eq:corner-reverse}
\end{align}
The first says the corner is almost fixed, by the Kazhdan gap.  The
second is the finite-stage form of $P=\pi(s)P\pi(s)^{*}$.  First the easy
containment $\opnorm{(1-Q_n)P_n}\le\varepsilon$: for $a\in S$ the
one-sided relation gives $sas^{-1}=\delta$ for a single $\delta\in L$,
and
\[
 (1-\Ad U_{a,n})\,\Ad U_{s,n}^{*}P_n
 =\Ad U_{s,n}^{*}\,(1-\Ad U_{\delta,n})\,P_n ,
\]
whose norm the Kazhdan gap makes small.  Each $\delta$ is a word in
$S$, and the gap controls the displacement it induces one letter at a
time, so this is one more threshold per generator --- $|S|$ of them,
all fixed before the stage is chosen.  So the corner lies almost
inside its image under $s$.  Then the equal-rank reversal estimate of
Section~\ref{app:finite-stage} turns that containment around: $Q_n$ is
a unitary conjugate of $P_n$ and so has the same rank, and for two
projections of equal finite rank a containment within $\varepsilon<1$
in one direction gives one within
$\varepsilon/\sqrt{1-\varepsilon^{2}}$ in the other.  That estimate rests on
finiteness rather than on any norm identity: the leakage bound makes the
compression $\operatorname{ran}P_n\to\operatorname{ran}Q_n$ injective,
and equal rank promotes an injective endomorphism of a
finite-dimensional space to a surjective one.  In an infinite-dimensional
space a proper isometry would block that promotion; equal finite rank
rules one out.  The one-sided inclusion $sLs^{-1}\le L$ enters as the
only group-theoretic input.
\leanverified{Sofic/InvolutionCollapseEndpoint}{GroupApproximation.InvolutionCollapseEndpoint.stage_transport_bound}

It remains to say what is compressed.  The object is not the
displacement map $b_n$, which is indexed by group elements and is not a
single matrix; it is a primitive for that map.  The Delorme--Guichardet
bound \eqref{eq:profile-bounded} on the limiting cocycle produces, at
each tolerance, a stage $n_0$ and a matrix $w_{n_0}$ with
$\lVert w_{n_0}\rVert_{\mathrm F}\le C+1$ satisfying
\[
 \bigl\lVert b_{n_0}(a)-\bigl(w_{n_0}
   -U_{a,n_0}w_{n_0}U_{a,n_0}^{*}\bigr)\bigr\rVert_{\mathrm F}\le\tau
 \qquad(a\in S),
\]
an approximate coboundary at one stage, for the finitely many
generators only.  Write $\widehat b_n=P_nw_nP_n$ for its compression
into the Kazhdan corner.  This is the step at which the limit is left
behind: the bound on $w_{n_0}$ comes from the limiting cocycle, and
everything after it is a statement about the single stage $n_0$.
\leanverified{Sofic/InvolutionCollapseCenter}{GroupApproximation.InvolutionCollapseCenter.exists_approximate_coboundary}

Compressing into the corner loses at most
$c_{\mathrm{cap}}=(C+1)\,2\tau/(1-\theta)$ in squared Frobenius norm,
and one
stage of transport bounds the displacement of $\widehat b_n$ by each
generator:
\[
 \operatorname{Tr}\bigl|\widehat b_n
   -U_{a,n}\widehat b_nU_{a,n}^{*}\bigr|^{2}
 \le 18q^{2}C^{2}+16c_{\mathrm{cap}}
 \le\Bigl(\frac\rho2\Bigr)^{2},
\]
the second inequality being the arithmetic of the choice of $q$ and
$\tau$.
\leanverified{Sofic/InvolutionCollapseEndpoint}{GroupApproximation.InvolutionCollapseEndpoint.numeric_transport_bound}
The primitive enters here, and it is the coboundary relation rather
than proximity that closes the argument: $b_n(a)$ does not approximate
$\widehat b_n$ --- the left side moves with $a$ and the right side does
not --- but approximates its displacement,
\[
 \bigl\lVert b_n(a)
   -\bigl(\widehat b_n-U_{a,n}\widehat b_nU_{a,n}^{*}\bigr)
 \bigr\rVert_{\mathrm F}\le\tau\le\frac\rho2
 \qquad(a\in S).
\]
Combined with the displayed transport bound this gives
$\|b_n(a)\|_{\mathrm F}\le\rho$ for every $a\in S$ at once, while
\eqref{eq:generator-mass} reads
$\sum_{a\in S}\|b_n(a)\|_{\mathrm F}^{2}=4$ and
$\sum_{a\in S}\rho^{2}=|S|/(\sqrt{|S|}+1)^{2}<1$: the single stage $n$
already fails.
\leanverified{Sofic/InvolutionCollapseEndpoint}{GroupApproximation.InvolutionCollapseEndpoint.anchor_contradiction}

The commutation hypothesis (W3) is
essential: it gives the joint spectral calculus behind the rank
identity of Step 2.  Anticommuting Clifford coordinates have no joint
spectral labels; they are instead collapsed by the central sign of
Theorem~\ref{thm:A}.  In $\mathcal Q$,
Theorem~\ref{thm:projection-collapse} below removes the restriction to
involutions, and Theorem~\ref{thm:torsion-collapse} extends the
collapse to witnesses of every finite order through their discrete
Fourier idempotents.
\end{remark}

The witness of Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse} is a group element.  The same
collapse holds for arbitrary projections of $\mathcal Q$.

\begin{theorem}[projection collapse]\label{thm:projection-collapse}
Let $H$ be countable, let $L\le H$ have property~\textup{(T)}, let
$s\in H$ satisfy $s\gamma s^{-1}\in L$ for every $\gamma\in L$, and let
$\Theta\colon H\to\U(\mathcal Q)$ be a homomorphism.  Let
$p\in\mathcal Q$ be a projection such that $p$ commutes with
$\Theta(s\gamma s^{-1})$ for every $\gamma\in L$ and the projections
$\Theta(\gamma)\,p\,\Theta(\gamma)^*$, $\gamma\in L$, commute pairwise.
Then $p$ commutes with $\Theta(L)$.
\leanverified{Sofic/ProjectionCompressionCollapse}{GroupApproximation.ProjectionCompressionCollapse.corona_projection_collapse}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The symmetry $\sigma=1-2p$ is a self-adjoint unitary of $\mathcal Q$.  The
subgroup $H'\le\U(\mathcal Q)$ generated by $\Theta(H)$ and $\sigma$ is
countable.  Inside $H'$, the image $\Theta(L)$ has
property~\textup{(T)}, $\Theta(s)$ conjugates it into itself, $\sigma$
is an involution centralizing the conjugate copy, and the
$\Theta(L)$-conjugates of $\sigma$
commute because the conjugated projections do.  So $\sigma$ is an
involutive orbit witness in $H'$, so by Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse}
every commutator $[\Theta(\gamma),\sigma]$, $\gamma\in L$, lies in
$\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(H')$.  The inclusion
$H'\to\U(\mathcal Q)$ is itself a homomorphism $H'\to\U(\mathcal Q)$, so it
maps each of these commutators to the identity:
$[\Theta(\gamma),\sigma]=1$ in $\mathcal Q$, and $\sigma=1-2p$ gives
$[\Theta(\gamma),p]_{-}=0$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:projection-collapse} applies to an arbitrary projection
of $\mathcal Q$, not only to one arising from a group element.  So
every projection $p\in\Theta(sLs^{-1})'\cap\mathcal Q$ whose
$\Theta(L)$-conjugates commute pairwise already lies in
$\Theta(L)'\cap\mathcal Q$.

If $k$ has finite order $m$, applying
Theorem~\ref{thm:projection-collapse} to the Fourier spectral
projections of $\Theta(k)$ removes the assumption that $k$ is an
involution.

\begin{theorem}[torsion collapse]\label{thm:torsion-collapse}
Let $H$ be countable, let $L\le H$ have property~\textup{(T)}, let
$s\in H$ satisfy $s\gamma s^{-1}\in L$ for every $\gamma\in L$, and
let $k\in H$ have finite order.  If the conjugate copy $sLs^{-1}$
centralizes $k$ and the conjugates $\gamma k\gamma^{-1}$,
$\gamma\in L$, commute pairwise, then every homomorphism
$\Theta\colon H\to\U(\mathcal Q)$ satisfies $\Theta([\gamma,k])=1$ for
every $\gamma\in L$; the
normal closure of all such commutators, over all finite-order witnesses
for $(L,s)$, lies in $\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(H)$, with
equality whenever the corresponding quotient of $H$ is MF.
\leanverified{Sofic/TorsionSpectralCollapse}{GroupApproximation.TorsionSpectralCollapse.actualCoronaMFInvisible_of_torsionWitness}
\leanverified{Sofic/TorsionSpectralCollapse}{GroupApproximation.TorsionSpectralCollapse.torsionCollapseDefect_le_actualCoronaMFResidual_of_kazhdan}
\leanverified{Sofic/TorsionSpectralCollapse}{GroupApproximation.TorsionSpectralCollapse.actualCoronaMFResidual_eq_torsionCollapseDefect_of_kazhdan}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $\Theta$ be a homomorphism to $\U(\mathcal Q)$, $m$ the order of $k$, and
$u=\Theta(k)$, a unitary with $u^{m}=1$.  Its discrete Fourier
idempotents
$p_j=m^{-1}\sum_{t\in\mathbf{Z}/m}e^{-2\pi ijt/m}u^{t}$, for
$j\in\mathbf{Z}/m$, are self-adjoint
idempotents of $\mathcal Q$.  Each $p_j$ is a polynomial in $u$, so
whatever commutes with $u$ commutes with every $p_j$: the represented
conjugate copy centralizes each $p_j$, and the
$\Theta(L)$-conjugates of each $p_j$ commute pairwise because the
conjugated witnesses do.  Theorem~\ref{thm:projection-collapse}
therefore makes every $p_j$ commute with $\Theta(L)$, and Fourier
reconstruction expresses $u=\sum_j e^{2\pi ij/m}p_j$,
so $u$ commutes with $\Theta(L)$ and
$\Theta([\gamma,k])=1$ for every $\gamma\in L$.
\end{proof}

In the quotient of the Kazhdan--Clifford group of
Theorem~\ref{thm:A} by its central involution the Clifford generators
commute, and the moved base-point generator is itself an involutive
witness.

\begin{theorem}[the commuting-generator quotient]\label{thm:commutinglamp}
The quotient $W/\langle\zeta\rangle$ of the Kazhdan--Clifford group
by its central involution is sofic and is not MF\@.  More precisely, the
image of the
commutator $[v_1,tct^{-1}]$, the inverse of the distinguished commutator
$u$, in $W/\langle\zeta\rangle$ is nontrivial and lies in
$\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(W/\langle\zeta\rangle)$; that last
conclusion holds with $W$ replaced by the ambient affine--Clifford group of
any injective endomorphism $\alpha$ of a countable
property-\textup{(T)} group and any base element outside the image of
$\alpha$.
\leanverified{Sofic/CommutingLampQuotientSofic}{GroupApproximation.CommutingLampQuotientSofic.literalSignFreeQuotient_isSofic_and_not_isCDEOperatorMF}
\leanverified{Sofic/CommutingLampCollapse}{GroupApproximation.CommutingLampCollapse.commutingLampQuotient_collapse}
\leanverified{Sofic/CommutingLampCollapse}{GroupApproximation.CommutingLampCollapse.literalCommutingLampQuotient_collapse}
\leanverified{Sofic/CommutingLampCollapse}{GroupApproximation.CommutingLampCollapse.literalCommutingLampQuotient_not_isCDEOperatorMF}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $q\colon W\to W/\langle\zeta\rangle$ and
$k=q(tct^{-1})$, the moved base-point generator.  Since $q(\zeta)=1$, any
two Clifford generators commute in the quotient, so the $q(L)$-conjugation orbit of
$k$ commutes pairwise, where $L=q(\bar\Gamma)$ is the image of the
base copy; $k$ remains an involution, and the conjugate copy
$q(t)Lq(t)^{-1}$ centralizes $k$ because $q(t)Lq(t)^{-1}$ fixes the
moved point.  Property~\textup{(T)} passes to the image $L$, so
by Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse} every commutator
$[q(\bar v_1),k]$ lies in the MF residual of the quotient.  The commutator
remains nontrivial in the sign quotient: in the signed finite-support model of
$\Cl(X)$, the commutator is the product of the two distinct basis
elements at $\tau o$ and $j(\bar v_1)\tau o$, whose configuration
coordinate is nonzero, while every element of
$\langle\zeta\rangle$ has configuration coordinate zero.

The Clifford kernel becomes abelian in the quotient:
$\Cl(X)/\langle\zeta\rangle$ is the elementary abelian group
$\bigoplus_X\Z/2$ under the same $\Sigma$-action, so
$W/\langle\zeta\rangle\cong\bigl(\bigoplus_X\Z/2\bigr)\rtimes\Sigma$.
That kernel is again locally finite with finite level orbits, so the
finite-by-sofic exhaustion in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:E} proves
soficity.
\end{proof}

The same argument applies to the finitely presented group
$E/\langle w\rangle$.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:signfree}]
Write $q\colon E\to E/\langle w\rangle$, $\bar d=q(d)$, and
$\bar L=q(\iota(\mathcal B))$.  The element $\bar d$ is an involution
because $c^2=1$, and $q(t)\bar Lq(t)^{-1}$ centralizes $\bar d$
because $t\iota(\mathcal B)t^{-1}$ commutes with $c$ in $E$.
Property~\textup{(T)} passes to $\bar L$, and $q(t)$ conjugates it into
itself.
Conditions (W1) and (W2) of Definition~\ref{def:invwitness} therefore
hold.  It remains to verify (W3).

The linear generators $x,y,z$ commute with $d$, and $t$ carries each $v_i$ to
$v_i^2$, so $\iota(v_i^2)$ commutes with $d$ as well.  Hence
$\iota(g)\,d\,\iota(g)^{-1}$ depends only on the exponent vector of
the translation part of $g$ modulo $2$, which leaves eight conjugates,
indexed by $(\Z/2)^3$.  By Theorem~\ref{thm:kazhdan-clifford} the
distinguished word is $w=[d,\iota(v_1)\,d\,\iota(v_1)^{-1}]$, the commutator
attached to the class of $v_1$.  The six words
$x,x^2,z,yz,yx,y^2x$ in the linear generators
carry that class onto the six remaining nonzero
classes, and each commutes with $d$; conjugating the displayed
identity by the corresponding $\iota(r)$ exhibits every nonzero parity
commutator as a conjugate of $w$, while the zero class contributes
$[d,d]=1$.  Centrality of $w$ places all eight in $\langle w\rangle$,
and one further conjugation reduces an arbitrary pair of conjugates to
this case.  Thus $\bar d$ is an involutive orbit witness for
$(\bar L,q(t))$, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse}
$[q(\iota(v_1)),\bar d]\in
\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(E/\langle w\rangle)$.

Nontriviality passes through the homomorphism $E\to W$ of
Proposition~\ref{prop:witness}: it carries $[\iota(v_1),d]$ to the
product of the Clifford generators at the two distinct points $\tau o$
and $j(\bar v_1)\tau o$, and carries $\langle w\rangle$ into
$\langle\zeta\rangle$, whose configuration coordinate is zero.  Hence
$[\iota(v_1),d]\notin\langle w\rangle$, its image in
$E/\langle w\rangle$ is nontrivial, and
Proposition~\ref{prop:univquot} makes $E/\langle w\rangle$ non-MF\@.
Corollary~\ref{cor:pullback} identifies
$\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(E)$ with the $q$-preimage of
$\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(E/\langle w\rangle)$, so $u$ belongs
to it.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[collapse computes the residual]\label{cor:collapsequot}
In the setting of Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse}, write
$q\colon H\to H/D_{\mathrm{coll}}(L,s)$ for the quotient map.  Then,
unconditionally,
\[
  \operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(H)
  \;=\;
  q^{-1}\bigl(\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}
  (H/D_{\mathrm{coll}}(L,s))\bigr),
\]
and if $H/D_{\mathrm{coll}}(L,s)$ is MF, then
$\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(H)=D_{\mathrm{coll}}(L,s)$.
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptExactWrappers}{GroupApproximation.ManuscriptExactWrappers.manuscriptCollapseRadicalReduction}
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse} with
Corollaries~\ref{cor:pullback} and~\ref{cor:exactradical}.
\end{proof}

In the second case $H/D_{\mathrm{coll}}(L,s)$ is the universal MF
quotient of $H$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:univquot}): every homomorphism $H\to\U(\mathcal Q)$ factors through it, and it is MF.

\begin{theorem}[when the collapse subgroup is everything]\label{thm:saturation}
In the setting of Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse}:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Every homomorphism $f$ from $H$ to a countable MF group maps
every element of $D_{\mathrm{coll}}(L,s)$ to the identity.
\item If $D_{\mathrm{coll}}(L,s)=H$, then
$\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(H)=H$; every homomorphism from $H$
to a countable MF group is trivial; and if $H$ is nontrivial, then
$H$ is not MF.
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptExactWrappers}{GroupApproximation.ManuscriptExactWrappers.manuscriptDefectSaturation}
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(1)  By Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse} and Lemma~\ref{lem:portable},
\[
 f(D_{\mathrm{coll}}(L,s))\le
 f(\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(H))\le
 \operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(f(H)).
\]
The subgroup $f(H)$ of the MF target is MF
(Lemma~\ref{lem:permanence}(1)), so its residual is trivial by
Proposition~\ref{prop:univquot}.
(2)  The residual statement is Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse} with
$D_{\mathrm{coll}}(L,s)=H$; triviality of every homomorphism follows
from (1); and a nontrivial group with full residual is not MF by
Proposition~\ref{prop:univquot}.
\end{proof}

For a group as in (2), every finite-dimensional unitary
representation is trivial, every MF quotient is trivial, and every
homomorphism to a countable residually finite group is trivial.
Whether a
small-cancellation construction produces a finitely presented group
with $D_{\mathrm{coll}}(L,s)=H$, or with a normal-Kazhdan defect equal
to the whole group, is open.


\section{Group algebras, soficity, and permanence}
\label{sec:algebras-sofic}

\subsection{The reduced group algebra of \texorpdfstring{$E$}{E}}

The stable finiteness in Theorem~\ref{thm:D} comes from two standard
facts.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:faithfultrace}
Let $G_1$ be a countable discrete group.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The canonical tracial state $\tau$ on $\Cred(G_1)$ is
faithful.
\item Every unital $C^*$-algebra $A$ with a faithful tracial state
is stably finite: for every $k\ge1$, every isometry in $M_k(A)$ is a
unitary.
\leanverified{Sofic/ManuscriptExactWrappers}{GroupApproximation.ManuscriptExactWrappers.manuscriptFaithfulTraceAndStableFiniteness}
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
(1) Realize $\Cred(G_1)\subseteq B(\ell^2G_1)$ as the norm closure
of the span of the left translation unitaries $\lambda_g$.  Let
$\rho_g$ be the right translation unitaries,
$(\rho_g\xi)(h)=\xi(hg)$.  Each $\rho_g$ commutes with each
$\lambda_{g'}$, hence with every element of $\Cred(G_1)$.  The
canonical trace is $\tau(x)=\langle x\delta_e,\delta_e\rangle$.  If
$\tau(x^*x)=0$ then $\lVert x\delta_e\rVert=0$, so $x\delta_e=0$,
and then
$x\delta_g=x\rho_{g^{-1}}\delta_e=\rho_{g^{-1}}x\delta_e=0$ for
every $g\in G_1$.  The vectors $\delta_g$ are total in
$\ell^2G_1$, so $x=0$.

(2) On $M_k(A)$ the functional
$\tau_k\bigl([x_{ij}]\bigr)=\tfrac1k\sum_i\tau(x_{ii})$ is a state
with the trace property
$\tau_k(xy)=\tfrac1k\sum_{i,j}\tau(x_{ij}y_{ji})=\tau_k(yx)$, and it
is faithful: the $i$-th diagonal entry of $x^*x$ is
$\sum_jx_{ji}^*x_{ji}$, so $\tau_k(x^*x)=0$ implies
$\tau(x_{ji}^*x_{ji})=0$, hence $x_{ji}=0$, for all $i,j$.  If
$v\in M_k(A)$ satisfies $v^*v=1$, then $y:=1-vv^*$ is a projection
with
$\tau_k(y)=\tau_k(1)-\tau_k(vv^*)=\tau_k(1)-\tau_k(v^*v)=0$; since
$y=y^*y$, faithfulness gives $y=0$, i.e.\ $vv^*=1$.
\end{proof}


\subsection{A sofic non-MF group}


\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:E}]
Write $T=\varinjlim(\bar\Gamma,\bar\alpha)$, so
$\Sigma=T\rtimes\mathbb Z$, and let $B\le\Sigma$ be the level-zero copy of
$\bar\Gamma$.  The image of $\bar\alpha$ has finite index (at most eight,
by the parity classes of the translation lattice).  Thus every
level $\bar\Gamma_n\le T$ is commensurable with $B$.  The stable letter
conjugates $B$ onto $\bar\alpha(\bar\Gamma)$, so all of $\Sigma$ commensurates
$B$.  Orbit--stabilizer now shows that every $\bar\Gamma_n$-orbit in
$X=\Sigma/B$ is finite.

Consider a finite subset of $\Cl(X)\rtimes T$.  Its $T$-coordinates
lie in one level $\bar\Gamma_n$.  Each of its Clifford coordinates has
finite $\bar\Gamma_n$-orbit: this holds for the central sign and for
each generator $c_x$, hence for every Clifford word.  The finitely many
resulting orbits
generate a finite $\bar\Gamma_n$-invariant subgroup $L\le\Cl(X)$,
because $\Cl(X)$ is locally finite.  Our original finite set therefore
lies in $L\rtimes\bar\Gamma_n$, which is sofic (finite-by-sofic).  A
group is sofic as soon as each of its finitely generated subgroups is,
so $\Cl(X)\rtimes T$ is sofic.

Reassociating the semidirect products gives
\[
 W=\Cl(X)\rtimes(T\rtimes\mathbb Z)
   \cong (\Cl(X)\rtimes T)\rtimes\mathbb Z.
\]
Thus $W$ is sofic, because a split extension of a sofic group by
$\Z$ is sofic.  Truncate the integer levels to $\Z/N$ and let the
shift act cyclically.  Induce the kernel's sofic approximations over
this finite level set.  The cocycle identity that makes the
finite-quotient case exact now fails only on the wrap-around boundary.
For a window shifted by $k$, that boundary occupies at most $2|k|/N$
of the levels, which vanishes as $N$ grows.
This is the $\Z$-extension case of the Elek--Szab\'o amenable-extension
theorem \cite{ElekSzabo}.  Here
$\bar\Gamma$ is sofic: its six generators lie in $\GL_4(\mathbb Z)$,
the resulting integral subgroup is residually finite through its
congruence quotients, and it is isomorphic to $\bar\Gamma$.

The map $E\to W$ of Proposition~\ref{prop:witness} is surjective: its image
contains $\Sigma$ and the generator $c_o$ at the base point, hence
every Clifford generator by transitivity of
the $\Sigma$-action on $X$.  Since $E$ is generated by
  $v_1,x,y,z,t,c$ (by the Tietze calculation above), their six
images generate $W$.  The map sends
$w\in\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(E)$ to the
nontrivial sign $\zeta$; functoriality (Lemma~\ref{lem:portable}) makes
$\zeta\in\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(W)$.  Hence $W$ is not MF.
\end{proof}
\paragraph{Failure of extension permanence.}
MF groups are not closed under extensions.  More precisely, $W$ fits into
an exact sequence
\[
 1\longrightarrow\Cl(X)\longrightarrow W\longrightarrow\Sigma
 \longrightarrow1
\]
whose kernel and quotient are MF, while $W$ is not.
The Clifford kernel is locally finite, LEF, sofic, and MF, while the
group $W$ is finitely generated and non-MF.
\leanverified{Sofic/LiteralWitnessConsequences}{GroupApproximation.LiteralWitnessConsequences.literalWitness_locallyFiniteKernel_nonMF}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:permanence}(3), the Clifford group is locally
finite, hence MF\@.  The group $\Sigma$ identifies with the subgroup of
$\GL_4(\mathbb Q)$ generated by $\bar\Gamma$ and $D$: the levels
are $D^{-n}\bar\Gamma D^{n}$, and the determinant gives the
$\mathbb Z$-coordinate.  It is residually finite.  At every odd
modulus the determinant $8$ of the dilation is invertible, so the
congruence reduction of the integral affine model descends to
conjugation by the dilation and extends over the direct limit and its
shift.  These reductions separate any nontrivial element; compare
Mal'cev's theorem \cite{Malcev} for finitely generated linear groups.  Thus $\Sigma$ is MF by Lemma~\ref{lem:permanence}(2),
whereas $W$ is not MF by the preceding construction.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Extensions by $\Z$.}
Composing the two
canonical projections gives a surjection $W\to\Z$ whose kernel is
$\Cl(X)\rtimes T$, and this kernel is LEF, hence MF: every finite
subset lies in $L\rtimes\bar\Gamma_n$ for a finite invariant subgroup
$L\le\Cl(X)$ and one level, and that group is residually
finite because the level acts on the finite group $L$ through a finite
quotient.  Thus $W$ is an extension of an LEF group by $\Z$ and is not
MF, while soficity is preserved by every extension with amenable
quotient, as in Elek--Szab\'o \cite{ElekSzabo}.
\leanverified{Sofic/LEFMarkedCompression}{GroupApproximation.LiteralLEFExtension.literalWitness_LEFKernel_integerExtension_nonMF}
\leanverified{Sofic/SoficByAmenablePermanence}{GroupApproximation.SoficByAmenablePermanence.manuscriptSoficOfSoficKerOfAmenableQuotient}

\paragraph{An exact stably finite non-MF algebra.}
The algebra $\Cred(W)$ is separable, exact, stably finite, and not
MF\@.  Indeed, the locally finite Clifford kernel is amenable and hence exact,
while the linear group $\Sigma\leq\GL_4(\mathbb Q)$ is exact
\cite{GHW}.  Exact groups are closed under extensions
\cite{KWPermanence}, so $W$ is exact; for discrete groups this is equivalent
to exactness of the reduced group algebra \cite{KWExact}.  The canonical trace
and stable finiteness follow as in Lemma~\ref{lem:faithfultrace}.  Finally, an
MF embedding of $\Cred(W)$ would restrict to a faithful homomorphism to $\U(\mathcal Q)$ on
the canonical group unitaries, contradicting that $W$ is not MF\@.

\paragraph{A simple sofic envelope.}
There is a countable simple sofic group $S_{\mathrm{simp}}$ with
\[
 \operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(S_{\mathrm{simp}})=S_{\mathrm{simp}}.
\]
Equivalently, every homomorphism from $S_{\mathrm{simp}}$ to an MF group is
trivial.

\begin{proof}
By Elek--Szab\'o, every countable sofic group embeds in a countable simple
sofic group \cite[Theorem~1]{ElekSzaboHyper}.  Embed $W$ in such a group.  Functoriality
of the MF residual sends $\zeta$ to a nontrivial element of the
MF residual of the simple group.  That residual is normal, so it is the whole
group.  Every homomorphism to an MF group maps the MF residual to $1$.
\end{proof}


\section{Marked limits, quasi-identities, and undecidability}
\label{sec:marked-horn}

\subsection{Marked-limit closure}

At every fixed number of marked generators the MF property is closed
under limits of marked groups, and its failure has a finite
certificate.

\begin{theorem}[the MF locus is closed]\label{thm:markedclosed}
Fix $k\ge1$ and consider the space of $k$-marked groups --- quotients
of the free group $F_k$, topologized so that a basic clopen set fixes
the truth of finitely many equations among words in the generators.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The marked groups with MF quotient form a closed set.
\item Equivalently, the non-MF marked groups form an open set.
\item Every non-MF $k$-marked group admits a finite-radius
certificate: there is a radius $R$ such that every $k$-marked group
whose relations agree with it on the reduced words of length at most
$R$ is non-MF.
\leanverified{Sofic/MarkedMFClosed}{GroupApproximation.MarkedGroupSpace.isClosed_operatorMFLocus}
\leanverified{Sofic/MarkedMFClosed}{GroupApproximation.MarkedGroupSpace.isOpen_compl_operatorMFLocus}
\leanverified{Sofic/MarkedMFClosed}{GroupApproximation.MarkedGroupSpace.exists_wordBall_cylinder_subset_compl_operatorMFLocus}
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A failure of MF for the quotient is a failure of normalized local
approximation, and such a failure is witnessed on one finite subset of
the quotient.  Expressing the finitely many multiplications and
inequalities of that subset by words in the marked generators produces
finitely many relation constraints; every marked group agreeing on
those constraints inherits the same failed local-model problem,
so an entire basic clopen neighborhood fails MF\@.  Enclosing the
constraint words in a reduced-word ball gives the radius form.
\end{proof}

\subsection{A finite universal Horn obstruction}

Every MF group satisfies a single finite quasi-identity: a universal
implication between finitely many equations, that is, a universal Horn
sentence.  Write $R$ for the forty-one relators of \eqref{eq:E},
regarded as words in eight variables, and $w$ for the distinguished word in those
variables.

\begin{proposition}[finite quasi-identity]\label{prop:horn}
Every MF group $G$ satisfies the finite universal Horn sentence
\[
 \forall x_1,\dots,x_8\colon\quad
 \bigwedge_{r\in R}\,r(x_1,\dots,x_8)=1
 \;\Longrightarrow\; w(x_1,\dots,x_8)=1 ,
\]
while the canonical generating tuple of $E$ satisfies every relator in $R$
and has $w\ne1$.
\leanverified{Sofic/LiteralUniversalHorn}{GroupApproximation.LiteralUniversalHorn.manuscriptLiteralUniversalHorn}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
A tuple satisfying the relators induces a homomorphism $E\to G$ sending
the distinguished word of $E$ to $w(x_1,\dots,x_8)$.  If $G$ is MF, that image
is trivial: $w\in\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(E)$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:A}, and the residual is functorial
(Lemma~\ref{lem:portable}).
The canonical tuple of $E$ satisfies $R$ by definition, and its
distinguished word is nontrivial by Theorem~\ref{thm:A}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[a basic clopen set of non-MF marked groups]\label{cor:cylinder}
In the space of eight-generator marked groups --- quotients of the free
group $F_8$, topologized so that a basic clopen set fixes the truth of
finitely many equations among words in the generators --- the marked groups
satisfying all relators of $R$ and $w\ne1$ form a nonempty clopen set
consisting entirely of non-MF groups.
\leanverified{Sofic/LiteralMarkedCylinder}{GroupApproximation.LiteralMarkedCylinder.literal_nonempty_clopen_nonMF_cylinder}
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Both conditions constrain finitely many values of the marked
multiplication, so the set is clopen; it contains the marked group $E$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:A}, and Proposition~\ref{prop:horn} excludes every MF
point.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Undecidability of MF recognition}

Lemma~\ref{lem:permanence}(1) and Theorem~\ref{thm:A} make MF a Markov
property of finitely presented groups: the trivial group is MF, and by
subgroup heredity the finitely presented group $E$ embeds in no MF
group.  The Adian--Rabin theorem \cite{Rabin58} therefore applies, and
classically MF recognition is undecidable.

Fix an effective coding of finite presentations by natural numbers,
under which a code determines computably its number of generators and
its finite list of relator words, and write $G_e$ for the group
presented by the code $e$.  The corollary below states the reduction
step alone: the computable map from halting instances to presentation
codes is a hypothesis of the statement rather than something
constructed here, its construction being the Adian--Rabin argument.

\begin{corollary}[undecidability, given a halting reduction]%
\label{cor:undecidable}
Suppose given a computable map $e$ sending each instance $i$ of the
halting problem to a presentation code $e(i)$ such that $G_{e(i)}$ is
MF exactly when $i$ halts.
Then no algorithm decides, from a finite presentation, whether the
presented group is MF, and the set of presentation codes of non-MF
groups is not recursively enumerable.
\end{corollary}
\leanverified{Computability/HaltingReduction}{GroupApproximation.MarkovMFConsequences.presentationCodes_recognition_undecidable_of_haltingReduction}
\leanverified{Computability/HaltingReduction}{GroupApproximation.MarkovMFConsequences.presentationCodes_negative_side_not_re_of_haltingReduction}

Effectiveness of the coding is necessary: a map attaching a finitely
presented group to each code need not be computable, and an
uncomputable choice would make the statement true for reasons having
nothing to do with groups.

The corollary reduces from the halting problem, whose undecidability
and non-recursively-enumerable complement are classical, so the fact
that some finitely presented group has unsolvable word problem is not
needed: the reduction asks only for a computable map and a correctness
equivalence, and does not require its source to be a word problem.
Constructing such a map is the Adian--Rabin argument
\cite{Rabin58} run over a group built from a machine; classically one
takes the word problem of a finitely presented group with unsolvable
word problem and applies Adian--Rabin to the Markov property ``is
MF''\@.  That construction fixes one finite presentation $P_0$ whose
group has undecidable word problem, and gives a computable map taking
a word $w$ in the generators of $P_0$ --- the instance varies, the
presentation does not --- to a finite presentation whose
group is trivial --- hence MF --- when $w=1$ in $P_0$, and contains $E$ ---
hence non-MF, by subgroup heredity --- when $w\ne1$.  The words
representing $1$ form a recursively enumerable set which, the word problem
of $P_0$ being undecidable, is not recursive; hence the words with $w\ne1$
form a set that is \emph{not} recursively enumerable.  An enumeration
of the non-MF presentations would enumerate that set, making the word
problem decidable.
\leanverified{Computability/MarkovMFConsequences}{GroupApproximation.MarkovMFConsequences.operatorMF_subgroup_hereditary}
\leanverified{Computability/MarkovMFConsequences}{GroupApproximation.MarkovMFConsequences.exists_finitelyPresented_nonOperatorMF}
\leanverified{Computability/MarkovMFConsequences}{GroupApproximation.MarkovMFConsequences.operatorMF_recognition_undecidable}
Finitely presented non-MF groups occur in infinite multiplicity, and
finitely generated ones in continuum multiplicity.
The groups $E\times\mathbb Z^{k}$ for $k\ge0$ are
finitely presented, pairwise nonisomorphic, and non-MF, each containing
$E$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:permanence}(1)).  They are pairwise nonisomorphic
because their abelianizations have distinct torsion-free ranks; the
number of homomorphisms to $\mathbb Z/2$ separates them as well.
\leanverified{Sofic/ProductMultiplicity}{GroupApproximation.ProductMultiplicity.manuscriptInfiniteMultiplicity}
Beyond finite presentation, pairing $E$
with a continuum of pairwise nonisomorphic finitely generated
groups yields $2^{\aleph_0}$ pairwise
nonisomorphic finitely generated non-MF groups: each product
$E\times N$ is non-MF, and a fixed countable group $X$ satisfies
$X\cong E\times N$ for at most countably many of the continuum many
isomorphism types $N$ --- each such $N$ embeds in $X$ as a finitely
generated subgroup, a countable group has only countably many finitely
generated subgroups, and the types $N$ are pairwise nonisomorphic.  A
map with countable fibres from a continuum-sized family has
continuum-sized image.  Products of pairwise nonisomorphic groups with
a fixed factor can still be isomorphic, so it is the isomorphism types
of the products that are counted.  The deduction uses only that the $N$
are finitely generated; a continuum of pairwise nonisomorphic finitely
generated groups is given by the lamplighters over coset spaces of the
free group, or by Neumann's two-generator groups \cite{Neumann37}.
\leanverified{Sofic/ContinuumMultiplicity}{GroupApproximation.ContinuumMultiplicity.manuscriptContinuumMultiplicity}
\leanverified{Monsters/NeumannContinuum}{GroupApproximation.NeumannContinuum.manuscriptContinuumMultiplicityUnconditional}

\section{Limitations of the operator-norm method}\label{sec:limits}

The proof is specific to the operator norm.  If multiplication
is controlled only in normalized Hilbert--Schmidt norm, the induced adjoint
operators on the Hilbert--Schmidt spaces need not be close in operator norm.
Both proofs of Theorem~\ref{thm:kazhdan-transport} use that operator-norm
control: without it the classes $[\Ad U_n(g)]_\omega$ need not be
multiplicative, and the spectral estimates of
Section~\ref{app:finite-stage} are unavailable.
The failure is exact and dimension-free: against
the operator-norm estimate $\opnorm{\Ad(U)-\Ad(V)}\le2\opnorm{U-V}$,
there are unitaries of arbitrarily small normalized Hilbert--Schmidt
distance whose adjoints stay $2$ apart in operator norm --- the same
constant, in the opposite direction.
\leanverified{Sofic/HilbertSchmidtAdjointGap}{GroupApproximation.HilbertSchmidtAdjointGap.exists_hsClose_adjointFar}

Conversely, amplification by an identity block separates the two
normalizations exactly.  Padding a model with an identity
block preserves every operator-norm quantity --- the multiplicative
defects and the pairwise separations are unchanged --- while the
normalized Hilbert--Schmidt distances decay with the padding ratio:
below any prescribed bound, every approximate unitary representation can be
replaced by one all of whose pairwise Hilbert--Schmidt distances are
smaller than the bound.
\leanverified{Sofic/NormTraceGap}{GroupApproximation.NormModel.exists_hs_collapse}
$2$-norm triviality of an element therefore does not by itself
prove a group non-MF: an obstruction must produce an invariant
corner carrying its own normalized trace, so that the normalization is
unchanged by amplification, rather than reading a normalized trace off
the ambient blocks.  The relative rank of that
substructure is unconstrained: padding
leaves the averaging corner $q_nM_{d_n}(\C)q_n$ untouched while $d_n$
grows, so $r_n/d_n$ may tend to zero while the
argument uses the corner's own normalized trace; the spectral
projection $q_n$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:normal-kazhdan} is normalized on
its own range in the same way, and the collapse argument of
Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse} normalizes by the rank $k_n$.

Alekseev--Thom ask
whether commutants arising in sofic approximations of Kazhdan groups
can be recovered from finite-dimensional coordinate subalgebras
\cite[Open Problem~6.2]{AlekseevThom}; that question is independent of
Theorem~\ref{thm:kazhdan-transport}.  The adjoint spectral estimates
used here need operator-norm multiplicative control, which permutation
and Hilbert--Schmidt models do not supply, so they settle neither
soficity nor hyperlinearity of $E$ or of $W$; soficity of both is
proved instead in Theorems~\ref{thm:E} and~\ref{thm:Esofic}.

Finite dimensionality of the coordinates cannot be replaced by stable
finiteness of the coordinate algebras together with faithful traces.
For faithfully traced unital $C^*$-coordinates $A$, the left and right
actions on $L^2(A,\tau)$ generate a von Neumann algebra that need not be
finite: for an infinite-dimensional $\mathrm{II}_1$ factor in standard
form it is all of $B(L^2(A,\tau))$.  Constant coordinates $A_n=\Cred(E)$ give a
counterexample to any such generalization: the left regular
representation embeds $E$ exactly and injectively into the norm
quotient
\[
 \textstyle\prod_nA_n\big/\bigoplus_nA_n ,
\]
the element $c$ centralizes the Kazhdan base there, and conjugation by
$t$ produces $tct^{-1}$, whose commutator with the base is the element
$u$ with $u^2=w\ne1$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:A}.
\leanverified{Sofic/FaithfullyTracedCoordinateNoGo}{GroupApproximation.FaithfullyTracedCoordinates.manuscriptFaithfullyTracedCoordinateNoGo}
Precisely: Theorem~\ref{thm:kazhdan-transport} becomes false when its
matrix coordinates are replaced by stably finite unital $C^*$-algebras
carrying faithful tracial states and its normalized Hilbert--Schmidt
norm by the trace $2$-norm, with all its remaining hypotheses
unchanged.

\subsection*{Questions}
Every finite subgroup of a torsion-free group is trivial, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion} is empty for such a group.
\leanverified{Sofic/TorsionFreeFiniteNormalLimit}{GroupApproximation.finiteSubgroup_eq_bot_of_isMulTorsionFree}
In Theorem~\ref{thm:normal-kazhdan} a normal
property-\textup{(T)} subgroup of $N_{\mathrm{conj}}$ obstructs
homomorphisms to $\U(\mathcal Q)$ with no torsion anywhere.  What remains
for a torsion-free example is group-theoretic --- a torsion-free finitely
presented group whose subgroup $N_{\mathrm{conj}}$ contains a normal
subgroup with property~\textup{(T)} --- and
Remark~\ref{rem:ff-realization} gives a small-cancellation route to
such a group, over the torsion-free finitely presented Kazhdan group
of \cite{FFF}, which removed the analogous torsion obstacle for
nonsoficity.
\begin{enumerate}
\item What is $\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(E)$?  It properly
contains $\{1,w\}$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:signfree}.  Write
$q_1\colon E\to E/\langle w\rangle$, let $D=D_{\mathrm{coll}}(\bar
L,q_1(t))$ for the witness $\bar d$ of that proof, and let
$q_2\colon E/\langle w\rangle\to(E/\langle w\rangle)/D$.  By
Corollary~\ref{cor:collapsequot} applied to $E/\langle w\rangle$ and
then Corollary~\ref{cor:pullback},
\[
 \operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(E)
 \;=\;q_1^{-1}q_2^{-1}
   \bigl(\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}
   ((E/\langle w\rangle)/D)\bigr),
\]
so by Corollary~\ref{cor:exactradical} the residual equals
$q_1^{-1}(D)$ exactly when $(E/\langle w\rangle)/D$ is MF\@.  Whether
it is, is open, and neither collapse theorem applies to it: $w$ is
already trivial in $E/\langle w\rangle$, and the commutators
generating $D$ are already trivial in the second quotient.
By Theorem~\ref{thm:B}, $\mathfrak D(E,\iota(\mathcal B))$ lies in the
kernel of every
finite-dimensional linear representation, and hence of every
homomorphism to a finite group; but after both quotients that
conclusion has no content unless some element of
$\mathfrak D(E,\iota(\mathcal B))$ has a nontrivial image there, and no
such element is exhibited here.  Deciding the question this way
therefore requires producing one.
\item Is there a torsion-free finitely presented non-MF group?  The
analytic obstruction no longer requires torsion
(Theorem~\ref{thm:normal-kazhdan}); what remains is to construct a
quotient
$q\colon E'\twoheadrightarrow Q$ of the ascending HNN extension $E'$
formed as in Theorem~\ref{thm:kazhdan-clifford} over Fournier-Facio's
torsion-free finitely presented property-\textup{(T)} group
\cite{FFF}, subject to six conditions: $Q$ is finitely presented;
$Q$ has property~\textup{(T)}; $Q$ is torsion-free; $Q$ is generated
by two elements; $q(N_{\mathrm{conj}})=Q$; and $q$ does not kill the
distinguished element of the datum, so that $Q\ne1$.

The last condition does not follow from the other five, and it is where
the difficulty lies.  The trivial quotient satisfies all five of the
others, whatever the underlying data: it is finitely presented, it has
property~\textup{(T)}, it is torsion-free, it is generated by two
elements, and it receives the conjugation defect onto all of itself.
It also has the entire profile listed next --- two-generated, finitely
presented, torsion-free, Kazhdan, and equal to its own MF residual ---
while being a counterexample to nothing.  So the first five conditions
are met for free, and what is open is meeting them with $Q\ne1$.
\leanverified{Sofic/FiveConditionInsufficiency}{GroupApproximation.exists_subsingleton_quotient}
\leanverified{Sofic/FiveConditionInsufficiency}{GroupApproximation.trivial_group_has_printed_profile}

Given such a $q$ with $Q\ne1$, the group $Q$ is two-generated, finitely
presented, torsion-free, Kazhdan, and equal to its own MF residual, so
every homomorphism from $Q$ to the unitary group of a matrix quotient is
trivial, and every nontrivial quotient of $Q$ likewise fails to be MF;
in particular $Q$ itself is not MF\@.
\leanverified{Sofic/FullMFRadicalEndpoint}{GroupApproximation.FullMFRadicalEndpoint.exists_nontrivial_twoGenerated_finitelyPresented_torsionFree_kazhdan_fullMFRadical}
\leanverified{Sofic/FullMFRadicalEndpoint}{GroupApproximation.FullMFRadicalEndpoint.exists_nontrivial_group_with_every_nontrivial_quotient_not_isCDEOperatorMF}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:saturation}, a group that equals its own collapse
defect admits no nontrivial homomorphism to any countable MF group at
all.  No
unconditional torsion-free finitely presented non-MF group is proved
here.  Hull's small-cancellation theorem for acylindrically hyperbolic
groups \cite[Theorem 1.5]{Hull} would give four of these five from a
two-generated \emph{suitable} subgroup $S$ of $N_{\mathrm{conj}}$ ---
one whose induced action is non-elementary and which normalizes no
finite subgroup --- since the quotient it returns is obtained by
adjoining finitely many relators, preserves element orders, and
restricts to a surjection from $S$, while property~\textup{(T)} descends
from a torsion-free Kazhdan lattice through a common quotient.  That
same order preservation would supply the sixth condition as well: a map
preserving element orders cannot send the distinguished element, which
has order greater than one, to the identity.
\leanverified{Sofic/FiveConditionInsufficiency}{GroupApproximation.orderPreserving_quotient_not_subsingleton}
What is
not available is a suitable such $S$: one infinite-order element of the
defect generating a hyperbolically embedded cyclic subgroup would supply
it \cite[Lemma 5.8]{Hull}, but the Bass--Serre action of an ascending
HNN extension fixes an end of its Bass--Serre tree, and an acylindrical action
is never quasi-parabolic \cite[Theorem~1.1]{OsinAH}, so that action does
not certify it.
\item Does MF imply hyperlinearity?  A faithful embedding into a matrix
quotient need not make any coordinate trace faithful, so the defining
MF representation does not immediately give a hyperlinear model.  This
question concerns the CDE convention fixed above; for stronger
trace-controlled MF conventions the implication is built into the definition.
\item By Proposition~\ref{prop:sounditeration}, every family of
theorems placing subgroups in the MF residual generates, by iteration
through quotients, a closure contained in
$\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}$.  For which groups do the criteria of
Theorems~\ref{thm:criterion}, \ref{thm:normal-kazhdan},
\ref{thm:collapse}, and~\ref{thm:projection-collapse} compute the
residual exactly?
\item Does the obstruction survive when the matrix blocks are replaced
by other building blocks?  The proof of
Theorem~\ref{thm:kazhdan-transport} uses finite dimensionality of
$M_{d_n}(\C)$ twice: for the conjugation action on
$L^2(M_{d_n}(\C),\tr_{d_n})$, and for finiteness of the norm
ultraproduct.  Section~\ref{sec:limits} shows that faithful traces
alone do not replace it.  For which separable $C^*$-algebras $A_n$ of
real rank zero does every homomorphism
$H\to\U\bigl(\prod_nA_n/\bigoplus_nA_n\bigr)$ still kill $w$?  The
lifting of Lemma~\ref{lem:lift}, which is where the matrix structure
first enters, is a semiprojectivity question in the sense of Loring
\cite{Loring98}.
\end{enumerate}

\section{The maximal group \texorpdfstring{$C^*$}{C*}-algebra}\label{app:maxcstar}

For a countable group $H$, the maximal group $C^*$-algebra $\Cmax(H)$ is
the completion of the group ring $\C[H]$ in the norm
\[
 \lVert x\rVert_{\max}=\sup_\pi\lVert\pi(x)\rVert,
\]
the supremum taken over unitary representations of $H$; the supremum is
finite because $\lVert\pi(\sum_h\lambda_hh)\rVert\le\sum_h|\lambda_h|$.
Write $u_h$ for the canonical unitary of $h\in H$.

\begin{proposition}[the universal property]
\label{prop:maximal-cstar}
The canonical map $h\mapsto u_h$ is injective, and for every unital
$C^*$-algebra $B$ lying in a fixed universe that contains $H$, and every
homomorphism $\rho\colon H\to\U(B)$, there is
a unique unital $*$-homomorphism $\Cmax(H)\to B$ with $u_h\mapsto\rho(h)$
for every $h\in H$.
\leanverified{Analysis/MaximalGroupCStar}{GroupApproximation.manuscriptUniverseRelativeMaximalGroupCStar}
\end{proposition}

Injectivity comes from the left regular representation, uniqueness from
density of $\C[H]$.  Restricting $B$ keeps ``all representations'' a
set rather than a proper class, and every target below is a unitary
group of a separable $C^*$-algebra.

Under a proper one-sided conjugation the maximal algebra contains an
isometry that is not unitary, while the reduced algebra of $E$ is
stably finite.

\begin{proposition}[a strictly dominated projection gives a proper isometry]\label{prop:proper-isometry}
Let $A$ be a unital $C^*$-algebra
containing a projection $p$ and a unitary $u$ such that the conjugate
$upu^{*}$ satisfies $p\,(upu^{*})=(upu^{*})\,p=p$, equivalently
$p\le upu^{*}$, and $upu^{*}\ne p$.  Then $A$
contains an isometry that is not a unitary; consequently $A$ is not
stably finite and has no faithful tracial state.
\leanverified{Analysis/ProperIsometryFromCompression}{GroupApproximation.ProperProjectionCompression.isometry_not_isUnit}
\leanverified{Analysis/ProperIsometryFromCompression}{GroupApproximation.ProperProjectionCompression.not_isStablyFiniteRing}
\leanverified{Analysis/ProperIsometryFromCompression}{GroupApproximation.ProperProjectionCompression.no_faithfulTracialState}
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}[the maximal algebra under proper one-sided conjugation]
\label{rem:maxinfinite}
Let $G$ contain a property-\textup{(T)} subgroup $\Gamma$ and an
element $t$ with $t\Gamma t^{-1}\subsetneq\Gamma$; the base of $E$ is
such a pair.  Let $P\in\Cmax(G)$ be the spectral projection at $1$ of
the Hermitian Kazhdan average $\tfrac1{2|S|}\sum_{s\in S}(u_s+u_s^{*})$,
which exists by the spectral gap of Section~\ref{sec:transport}.  Then
$P\le u_tPu_t^{*}$, and the inequality is strict: in the quasi-regular
representation on $\ell^2(G/t\Gamma t^{-1})$ the point mass at the base
coset is fixed by $t\Gamma t^{-1}$ and moved by every element of
$\Gamma$ outside it, so the two projections differ there.
\leanverified{Analysis/QuasiRegularWitness}{GroupApproximation.QuasiRegularWitness.baseVector_apply_base}
\leanverified{Analysis/MaximalCStarParagraphEndpoint}{GroupApproximation.MaximalCStarParagraphEndpoint.manuscriptMaximalCStarStrictCompressionRemark}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:proper-isometry}, $\Cmax(G)$ is then not
stably finite, has no faithful trace, and in particular is neither
residually finite-dimensional nor MF\@.  For the reduced algebra of
$E$, by contrast, the canonical trace is faithful and the algebra is
stably finite (Theorem~\ref{thm:D}), so its failure of MF is not a
failure of finiteness.
\end{remark}

\section{The thirteen-relator presentation of \texorpdfstring{$\SL_3(\mathbb Z)$}{SL3(Z)}}\label{app:p13}

Property~\textup{(T)} of $\mathbb Z^3\rtimes\SL_3(\mathbb Z)$ is
classical \cite[Example~1.7.4(i)]{BHV}, and the presentations go back to
\cite[Theorem~2]{CRW}.  Proposition~\ref{prop:literal-base-T} derives
property~\textup{(T)} for the twenty relators of
\eqref{eq:gamma-presentation} through the thirteen-relator presentation
$P_{13}$, using the isomorphism $P_{13}\cong\SL_3(\mathbb Z)$ proved
here.

\begin{theorem}[completeness of the thirteen-relator presentation]
\label{thm:p13-complete}
The canonical homomorphism $P_{13}\to\SL_3(\mathbb Z)$, sending each
generator $e_{ij}$ to the corresponding elementary matrix, is an
isomorphism.
\leanverified{Monsters/P13DescentMaster}{GroupApproximation.P13DescentMaster.p13CompletenessEquiv}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $\pi\colon P_{13}\to\SL_3(\mathbb Z)$ for the map in question, and
\[
 \omega_{12}=e_{12}e_{21}^{-1}e_{12},\qquad
 \omega_{13}=e_{13}e_{31}^{-1}e_{13},\qquad
 \omega_{23}=e_{23}e_{32}^{-1}e_{23}
\]
for the three signed swaps; $\omega_{12}$ is the Weyl element of the
upper-left $2\times2$ block, and the thirteenth relator is
$\omega_{12}^4=1$.  Let $e_3$ be
the third basis column of $\mathbb Z^3$, let
$\lVert\cdot\rVert_1$ be the $\ell^1$ norm on $\mathbb Z^3$, and put
\[
 H_{\mathrm{par}}=\langle e_{12},e_{21},e_{31},e_{32}\rangle\le P_{13},
\]
the subgroup on the four root letters whose matrices fix $e_3$.

\emph{Surjectivity.}  Division with
remainder in the first column of $A\in\SL_3(\mathbb Z)$ is a product of
integer powers of elementary matrices, and iterating it on columns and
then on rows reduces $A$ to a diagonal sign matrix.  Each such sign
matrix is the square of a signed swap, and each signed swap is the
displayed three-transvection word.

\emph{Injectivity: a normal form.}  Write an element of $P_{13}$ as a
word $W=\ell_k\cdots\ell_1$ in the letters $e_i^{\pm1}$.  Given a
column $b\in\mathbb Z^3$, the \emph{trajectory} of $W$ over $b$ is the
sequence $b,\ \pi(\ell_1)b,\ \pi(\ell_2\ell_1)b,\ \dots,\ \pi(W)b$, and
$W$ is \emph{norm-monotone} over $b$ when the $\ell^1$ norms along that
sequence do not decrease.  Using only the thirteen relators, every $W$
can be rewritten as
\[
 W=G\,m,\qquad m\in\langle\omega_{13},\omega_{23}\rangle,\qquad
 G\ \text{norm-monotone over}\ b:=\pi(m)e_3 .
\]
The rewriting is a descent, in the lexicographic order on
$\mathbb N\times\mathbb N$, of the pair consisting of the largest
$\ell^1$ norm attained along the trajectory at a position where
monotonicity fails, and the number of positions at which it attains
that norm and fails.  One step splits $W$ at such a position and
rewrites the offending pair of adjacent letters; the thirty-six ordered
pairs of root families fall into four classes --- cancelling,
commuting, Steinberg, and braid --- each handled by one of the
displayed relators, and each strictly decreasing the pair.  The
thirty-six cases are
enumerated in the supplementary material.

\emph{Injectivity: a kernel word has constant trajectory.}
Suppose $\pi(W)=1$.
The subgroup $\langle\omega_{13},\omega_{23}\rangle$ maps into the signed
permutation matrices, so $b=\pi(m)e_3$ is a signed basis column and
$\lVert b\rVert_1=1$.  The trajectory of $G$ over $b$ starts at $b$ and
ends at $\pi(G)b=\pi(m)^{-1}b=e_3$, again of norm one, and it is
nondecreasing; hence it is constant.  A root letter that preserves the
$\ell^1$ norm of a signed basis column fixes that column, so every
letter of $G$ fixes $b$ --- and, taking the whole word,
$\pi(m)e_3=\pi(G)b=e_3$, so $\pi(m)$ fixes $e_3$.

\emph{Injectivity: reduction to the parabolic subgroup.}
Conjugation by $\omega_{13}$ and by
$\omega_{23}$ permutes the twelve letters $e_i^{\pm1}$, by two explicit
tables derived from the relators.  Applying them letter by letter turns
$m^{-1}Gm$ into a word in root letters, each of which fixes
$\pi(m)^{-1}b=e_3$.  The root letters whose matrices fix $e_3$ are
exactly $e_{12}^{\pm1},e_{21}^{\pm1},e_{31}^{\pm1},e_{32}^{\pm1}$, so
$m^{-1}Gm\in H_{\mathrm{par}}$.

\emph{Injectivity: the subgroup $\langle\omega_{13},\omega_{23}\rangle$.}
The group $\langle\omega_{13},\omega_{23}\rangle$ acts on the six
signed basis columns,
and the orbit of $e_3$ is all six of them, with coset representatives
\[
 1,\quad \omega_{13},\quad \omega_{23},\quad \omega_{13}^2,\quad
 \omega_{13}^3,\quad \omega_{23}\omega_{13}^2
\]
carrying $e_3$ to $e_3,e_1,e_2,-e_3,-e_1,-e_2$ respectively.  Twelve
Schreier relations, one for each generator and coset, have the form
$\omega_\bullet\,r_j=r_{j'}\,\omega_{12}^{k}$: every Schreier element is a
power of the block Weyl element $\omega_{12}$; the twelve are listed in
the supplementary material.  The remaining Schreier relation is
$\omega_{23}^2=\omega_{12}^2\omega_{13}^2$.  In $K_2(3,\mathbb Z)$ the
Steinberg symbol $\{-1,-1\}$ is $\omega_{12}^{4}$, so this relation
holds in $P_{13}$ exactly because the thirteenth relator sets
$\omega_{12}^4=1$.  Hence
every element of $\langle\omega_{13},\omega_{23}\rangle$ is
$r_j\omega_{12}^k$; if in addition its
matrix fixes $e_3$, then $r_je_3=e_3$ forces $j=0$, leaving
$m=\omega_{12}^k\in
H_{\mathrm{par}}$.  With the previous step,
\[
 W=m\cdot\bigl(m^{-1}Gm\bigr)\in H_{\mathrm{par}} .
\]
The next step is applied to this product and not to $m^{-1}Gm$ alone,
whose image under $\pi$ is $\pi(m)^{-1}$ rather than $1$.

\emph{Injectivity: the parabolic subgroup.}  Let
$N=\langle e_{31},e_{32}\rangle$ and $\Lambda=\langle
e_{12},e_{21}\rangle$.  The Steinberg relations make $N$ abelian and
normalized by $\Lambda$, and every element of $H_{\mathrm{par}}$ is
$e_{31}^{a}e_{32}^{b}\gamma$ with $\gamma\in\Lambda$.  On $\Lambda$ the
displayed relators reduce to
\[
 \Lambda=\langle p,q\mid pqp=qpq,\ (pqp)^{4}=1\rangle,
 \qquad p=e_{12},\quad q=e_{21}^{-1},
\]
and $\pi$ carries $p$ and $q$ to the two elementary matrices of the
upper-left $\SL_2(\mathbb Z)$ block, whose entries outside that block
are those of the identity.  Put
\[
 \chi=pqp,\qquad \psi=pq,\qquad \eta=\chi^{2}.
\]
Since $p=\psi^{-1}\chi$ and $q=\chi^{-1}\psi^{2}$, the pair
$\chi,\psi$ generates $\Lambda$, and the braid relation is equivalent
to $\chi^{2}=\psi^{3}$; thus
$\Lambda=\langle\chi,\psi\mid\chi^{2}=\psi^{3},\ \chi^{4}=1\rangle$,
and $\eta=\chi^{2}=\psi^{3}$ is central with $\eta^{2}=1$.  Killing
$\eta$ leaves
$\langle\chi,\psi\mid\chi^{2}=\psi^{3}=1\rangle
=\mathbb Z/2\ast\mathbb Z/3$.  The M\"obius ping-pong argument
identifies $\mathrm{PSL}_2(\mathbb Z)$ with the free product of the
order-two and order-three subgroups generated by the images of
$\pi(\chi)$ and $\pi(\psi)$, so the induced surjection
$\Lambda/\langle\eta\rangle\to\mathrm{PSL}_2(\mathbb Z)$ carries each
free factor isomorphically onto the corresponding one and is therefore
an isomorphism.  Hence $\ker(\pi|_\Lambda)\subseteq\langle\eta\rangle$;
and $\pi(\eta)=\operatorname{diag}(-1,-1,1)\ne I$, so
$\ker(\pi|_\Lambda)=1$.  Now let
$W=e_{31}^{a}e_{32}^{b}\gamma\in H_{\mathrm{par}}$ have $\pi(W)=1$.
The upper-left $2\times2$ block of $\pi(W)$ is the matrix of $\gamma$,
so $\gamma=1$; the bottom row of $\pi(W)$ is then $(a,b,1)$, so
$a=b=0$.  Hence $W=1$, and $\ker\pi$ is trivial.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[the classical identification]\label{rem:classical-base}
The eight relations \eqref{eq:sl3presentation} present
$\SL_3(\mathbb Z)$: Theorem~\ref{thm:p13-complete} composed with the
surjection of Step 2 identifies $\mathcal R$ with $\SL_3(\mathbb Z)$,
and the base $\mathcal B$ is then the affine group
$\mathbb Z^3\rtimes\SL_3(\mathbb Z)$ --- the surjection
$\mathcal B\to\bar\Gamma$ of Lemma~\ref{lem:linear} onto the matrix
realization is an isomorphism.
\leanverified{Monsters/LiteralBaseCompleteness}{GroupApproximation.LiteralBaseCompleteness.baseAffineEquiv}
The presentations go back to \cite[Theorem~2]{CRW}, with the
matrix-labelled generators also recorded in \cite{CLV}, and
property~\textup{(T)} of the affine group is classical
\cite[Example~1.7.4(i)]{BHV}.
\end{remark}

